const BQ=[
{"id":"P1","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A telecom operator wants to automate change-of-address requests. Customers send free-form emails and scanned forms. The new address must be validated against the national postal database, and the customer record must be updated in the CRM. Which division of responsibilities best fits a Claude-based solution?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Claude handles each request from email to CRM update, including the final approval, because keeping the process inside one model call maximises automation and minimises handoffs."},{"l":"B","t":"Claude extracts the fields, checks the address against its own knowledge of postal codes, and writes the confirmed result straight into the CRM through a tool call."},{"l":"C","t":"A human agent retypes every request into the CRM, and Claude is limited to drafting the confirmation email so that no automated step can touch customer records."},{"l":"D","t":"Claude extracts the address fields; the postal database validates them; the CRM's existing API writes the update; low-confidence extractions are sent to a person."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Decompose into three owners. Claude does the language work (extraction), existing systems own what is already reliable (postal validation, CRM writes), and humans own exceptions. Letting Claude validate from memory or approve its own work over-assigns it tasks that systems of record handle better; the manual option under-uses Claude.","x":["Incorrect. One model call that also approves its own output removes the checks that matter. Postal validation and CRM writes belong to systems of record, and fewer handoffs do not justify dropping validation.","Incorrect. A model's memory of postal codes is not a source of truth, so it can accept an address that does not exist. The postal database must do the validation, and a confirmed value should not be written straight to the CRM on the model's word.","Incorrect. Retyping every request wastes Claude's strength at reading free-form emails and scanned forms. Using Claude for extraction does not require giving it unchecked access to customer records.","Correct. Claude does the language work, the postal database validates, the CRM's own API writes the update, and low-confidence cases go to a person. Each task sits with the owner that does it most reliably."],"df":"easy","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P3","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A platform team wants Claude to help diagnose intermittent production incidents. Each incident involves different services, logs, and metrics, so the investigation path cannot be written in advance. The assistant would have read-only access to logs and dashboards, and engineers act on its findings. Which design is most appropriate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A fixed workflow that runs the same predefined sequence of log queries for every incident, keeping behaviour predictable."},{"l":"B","t":"An agent with a constrained read-only tool set, a per-run turn and token budget, and explicit stopping criteria."},{"l":"C","t":"A single augmented call that receives a full day of raw logs in the prompt and returns a diagnosis in one pass."},{"l":"D","t":"A RAG pipeline over last year's postmortems that answers the incident question directly from similar past cases."}],"a":["B"],"r":"When the path cannot be enumerated and the cost of a wrong intermediate step is recoverable (read-only, human acts on the output), an agent is justified, but only with constrained tools, budgets, and stopping criteria. A fixed workflow cannot adapt, a single call cannot fit or navigate the logs, and past postmortems are a snapshot, not live state.","x":["Incorrect. A fixed sequence of queries cannot adapt when each incident involves different services, logs and metrics. The investigation path is unknown in advance, which is the case for an agent.","Correct. When the path cannot be written in advance and the tools are read-only with a human acting on the findings, an agent is justified. The tool limits, turn and token budget, and stopping criteria keep it bounded.","Incorrect. A full day of raw logs will not fit or be navigable in one prompt, and one pass cannot follow leads from one service to the next.","Incorrect. Past postmortems are a snapshot of old incidents, not live state. They can inform the investigation but cannot answer the current incident on their own."],"df":"medium","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P7","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Which failure in a multi-agent run is hardest to recover from, and which design response addresses it?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The orchestrator losing its goal or synthesis state; checkpoint its progress so a failed run can resume."},{"l":"B","t":"A subagent returning malformed output; give every subagent a larger context window so that malformed results stop occurring."},{"l":"C","t":"Traces fragmenting across agents; retry each subagent three times so that the traces line up again."},{"l":"D","t":"Two subagents returning conflicting results; propagate a shared trace identifier so that the conflict resolves itself."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Subagent failures and conflicts are caught at recoverable boundaries, but the orchestrator holds the goal and synthesis state, so losing it is often unrecoverable. Protect it with checkpoints. The other options pair a failure with a response that does not address it.","x":["Correct. The orchestrator holds the goal and synthesis state, so losing it is often unrecoverable. Checkpointing its progress lets a failed run resume.","Incorrect. A larger context window does not stop malformed output, and a malformed subagent result can be caught and recovered at the boundary.","Incorrect. Retrying subagents does not repair fragmented traces. Traces need consistent identifiers across agents.","Incorrect. Conflicting subagent results are recoverable at the boundary, and a shared trace identifier helps debugging but does not resolve the conflict."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design multi-agent systems and orchestration strategies"},
{"id":"P8","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A regulated lender is deciding whether an autonomous agent should adjudicate refund-dispute cases. Every decision must be reconstructable for regulators, a wrong decision triggers audits and remediation, the deadline is hours rather than seconds, and the team can enumerate the steps for the common case. Which TWO factors most directly rule out the agent pattern? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"**Model size:** agents require the largest model tier, which makes them unaffordable for the lender."},{"l":"B","t":"**Latency budget:** an agent loop cannot possibly finish within a few hours, so the deadline excludes it."},{"l":"C","t":"**Observability:** the agent's path is not written in advance, while the audit requirement needs steps that log like ordinary code."},{"l":"D","t":"**Error cost:** a wrong result across several unguarded turns is expensive, whereas a workflow can place deterministic guards between steps."},{"l":"E","t":"**Context window:** an agent cannot hold a single case file, so it would lose track of the dispute details."}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Apply the five factors in sequence. High error cost and a hard observability/audit requirement rule out an agent here. A multi-hour deadline is generous, agents do not inherently require the largest tier, and a case file fits comfortably in context.","x":["Incorrect. Agents do not inherently require the largest model tier, so affordability is not what rules the pattern out here.","Incorrect. A deadline of hours is generous for an agent loop, so latency does not exclude the agent.","Correct. The audit requirement needs every step reconstructable, and an agent's path is not written in advance. Steps that log like ordinary code come from a workflow.","Correct. A wrong decision triggers audits and remediation, and an agent can compound errors across unguarded turns. A workflow can place deterministic guards between steps.","Incorrect. A single case file fits comfortably in the context window, so the agent would not lose track of the dispute details."],"df":"hard","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P11","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A draft business case claims an AI contract-review assistant will cut senior-associate time by 60%. Which TWO weaknesses would a finance reviewer most likely identify? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The case states a payback period and shows how it changes if volume doubles."},{"l":"B","t":"The run cost is built from the token distribution produced by the sizing model."},{"l":"C","t":"The baseline is expressed in hours per contract, a unit the business recognises."},{"l":"D","t":"The projection treats the associate review step as eliminated although the design keeps the associate as final reviewer."},{"l":"E","t":"The baseline hours per contract were estimated from partners' intuition rather than measured."}],"a":["D","E"],"r":"Two common ROI-map errors are an estimated rather than measured baseline and assuming full automation when the design requires human review (labour is reduced, not removed). Using the sizing distribution, stating payback with sensitivity, and using a recognisable business unit are all good practice.","x":["Incorrect. Stating a payback period and showing sensitivity to volume is good practice, not a weakness.","Incorrect. Building the run cost from the token distribution of the sizing model is good practice.","Incorrect. A baseline in hours per contract is a unit the business recognises, which is a strength.","Correct. The projection treats the associate review step as eliminated although the design keeps the associate as final reviewer, so labour is reduced and not removed.","Correct. A baseline estimated from partners' intuition rather than measured weakens every figure built on it."],"df":"hard","ob":"Align solutions to business value pillars (efficiency, transformation, productivity, cost, performance SLAs)"},
{"id":"P13","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"An architecture sketch shows requests flowing from the user into a Claude workflow and out as an approved output delivered downstream. The team wants the design to improve over time. Which element completes the end-to-end loop?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Capture whether each output was accepted, revised, or rejected downstream and feed it into evals and monitoring."},{"l":"B","t":"Retrain the Claude model weekly on the outputs that were delivered, so that it learns the team's preferences."},{"l":"C","t":"Log only token counts per request, since whether an output was good is a question for the end user."},{"l":"D","t":"Increase the context window so that the model can remember its previous outputs across all later requests."}],"a":["A"],"r":"A feedback loop needs an outcome signal. Whether the output was accepted, revised, or rejected feeds evals and monitoring, which is how change is gated and drift is detected. Claude's weights are not retrained by your application, and token counts say nothing about quality.","x":["Correct. An outcome signal (accepted, revised or rejected downstream) is what closes the loop. Feeding it into evals and monitoring is how changes are gated and drift is detected.","Incorrect. An application does not retrain Claude's weights. Learning the team's preferences happens through prompts, evals and retrieval, not retraining.","Incorrect. Token counts say nothing about whether an output was good, so logging only them gives no quality signal. Quality needs an outcome measure.","Incorrect. A larger context window does not create a feedback loop. It would not give the system any signal on whether past outputs were good."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design end-to-end architectures (input → processing → output → feedback loops)"},
{"id":"P18","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A 40-person claims-operations team, none of them developers, needs to ask questions over an approved set of internal policy documents and share context with each other. Nothing in the request requires retrieving live records or embedding the assistant in another product. Which recommendation fits, and on which deciding tradeoff?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"claude.ai with a Project: the deciding tradeoff is audience, and this team needs a ready-made entry point."},{"l":"B","t":"An MCP server over the document store, because the deciding tradeoff is reuse across the team's future internal tools."},{"l":"C","t":"Claude Code on shared laptops, because the deciding tradeoff is audience and it runs wherever the team already works."},{"l":"D","t":"The direct API behind a custom-built interface, because the deciding tradeoff is integration depth for the policy documents."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Pick the entry point by the work it has to do. With no programmatic retrieval and nothing to embed, audience is the deciding tradeoff and a ready-made product wins. Claude Code is built for engineers, a custom build adds cost the use case does not need, and MCP's reuse argument does not apply when only one client will ever consume it.","x":["Correct. With no retrieval of live records and nothing to embed, the audience is what decides. A non-developer team needs a ready-made entry point, and a Project shares context.","Incorrect. MCP's advantage is reuse across clients, and only one client would use it here, so it adds work without a benefit.","Incorrect. Claude Code is built for engineers, and this team is not made up of developers.","Incorrect. A custom interface on the API adds build cost that the use case does not need."],"df":"medium","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P111","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"An HR team wants Claude to answer employee questions about leave entitlements. The answers live in 30 policy documents that are revised every quarter. Which design fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Train a custom model on the policy documents each quarter so that the current policy is stored in the model's weights."},{"l":"B","t":"Retrieve from the current policy documents at question time, cite the source section, and re-index when a policy changes."},{"l":"C","t":"Let Claude answer from its general knowledge of typical leave policies and add a disclaimer to every reply."},{"l":"D","t":"Build a fixed table of questions and canned answers and bypass the model for anything the table does not contain."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Frequently changing, document-based knowledge is a retrieval problem: fetch the current text, cite it, and re-index on change. Weights cannot be updated quarterly by the application, general knowledge is not the company's policy, and a canned table cannot cover free-form questions.","x":["Incorrect. The application cannot retrain the model quarterly, and weights would still lag behind each revision.","Correct. Retrieve the current policy text at question time, cite the source section, and re-index when a policy changes.","Incorrect. General knowledge of typical leave policies is not this company's policy, and a disclaimer does not make it right.","Incorrect. A fixed table of canned answers cannot cover free-form questions and bypasses the model's strengths."],"df":"easy","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P112","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A procurement team wants to match purchase orders to goods-receipt notes and flag mismatches. The matching rules are exact: item code, quantity, and unit price must agree within a stated tolerance. Which division of work is best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Claude extracts fields from the documents; code applies the matching rules; mismatches go to a person."},{"l":"B","t":"Claude reads both documents and decides whether they match, because it can reason about differences in wording."},{"l":"C","t":"Claude extracts the fields and also applies the tolerance rules, since splitting the steps adds handoffs."},{"l":"D","t":"A person keys both documents into the ERP system by hand, with Claude used only to summarise each mismatch."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Claude owns the language work (reading documents), code owns exact rules and tolerances, and a person owns mismatches. Asking Claude to apply exact tolerances is the over-assignment the decomposition is meant to avoid.","x":["Correct. Claude does the language work of extracting fields, code applies the exact matching rules and tolerances, and a person handles mismatches.","Incorrect. Exact item code, quantity and price matching within a stated tolerance is better done by code than by a model's judgement.","Incorrect. Asking Claude to apply exact tolerance rules over-assigns it a task that code does reliably, and the saved handoff is not worth the risk.","Incorrect. Manual keying of both documents wastes Claude's strength at reading documents and gives it only a minor role."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply decomposition techniques for complex problem solving"},
{"id":"P113","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A support inbox receives refund requests, technical faults, and legal complaints. Each category needs a different prompt, different tools, and a different escalation path. Which workflow shape fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"One long prompt that describes all three categories and lets the model improvise the handling for each message."},{"l":"B","t":"An autonomous agent that decides on its own which tools to use, with no fixed categories."},{"l":"C","t":"Routing: classify the message first, then send it to a specialised handler for that category."},{"l":"D","t":"Evaluator-optimizer: draft a reply and revise it repeatedly until a judge model is satisfied."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Distinct categories with different prompts, tools, and escalation paths is the textbook case for routing. One long prompt blurs the handling, an agent adds variance the task does not need, and evaluator-optimizer addresses output quality, not input variety.","x":["Incorrect. One long prompt blurs three different handling paths and relies on the model to improvise them.","Incorrect. An autonomous agent with no fixed categories adds variance the task does not need.","Correct. Routing classifies the message first and sends it to a specialised handler with its own prompt, tools and escalation.","Incorrect. Evaluator-optimizer improves output quality. It does not solve the variety of incoming categories."],"df":"medium","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P115","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A research agent sometimes loops for hundreds of turns, repeating similar searches and burning budget without converging. Which design response addresses the root cause?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Switch to a larger model, on the basis that a more capable agent will recognise sooner when it has already found the answer and stop by itself."},{"l":"B","t":"Add a turn and token budget with explicit stopping criteria, and return a partial result with a note when the budget is reached."},{"l":"C","t":"Remove the search tool after the tenth call so that the agent has to answer from what it already holds."},{"l":"D","t":"Ask the agent in its prompt to try to be efficient and to stop whenever it feels that it has enough."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Unbounded loops are a known agent failure. The fix is structural: budgets, stopping criteria, and a defined partial-result path. A larger model or a prompt request to be efficient does not enforce anything.","x":["Incorrect. A larger model does not enforce a stop. Without a limit it can still loop.","Correct. A turn and token budget with explicit stopping criteria, and a partial result with a note when the budget is hit, fixes the cause structurally.","Incorrect. Removing the search tool after ten calls is arbitrary and may stop an agent that was converging.","Incorrect. Asking the agent to be efficient in the prompt is a request, not an enforced limit."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design multi-agent systems and orchestration strategies"},
{"id":"P119","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Parallel subagents each append their findings to one shared file. Results are occasionally lost or interleaved. Which design change removes the problem?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell each subagent to wait a random number of seconds before writing so that the writes rarely coincide."},{"l":"B","t":"Have each subagent return its result to the orchestrator, which merges them; avoid shared mutable state between subagents."},{"l":"C","t":"Run all the subagents in a single shared conversation so that each one can see the others' edits to the file as they happen and avoid collisions."},{"l":"D","t":"Add a line to each subagent's prompt asking it to check the file for conflicts before it writes."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Shared mutable state between parallel workers produces races. Have subagents return results to the orchestrator, which merges them in one place. Delays and polite instructions do not remove the race.","x":["Incorrect. Random delays only make collisions less frequent. The shared file is still a race.","Correct. Subagents return results to the orchestrator, which merges them in one place. Without shared mutable state there is nothing to race on.","Incorrect. A shared conversation does not stop concurrent writes to the same file, and it adds noise to each subagent's context.","Incorrect. A check before writing is not atomic, so two subagents can still write at the same moment."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design multi-agent systems and orchestration strategies"},
{"id":"P120","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Which end-to-end flow best matches the ticket-triage reference architecture?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ingest, classify and extract fields, route to a queue, draft a reply, send low-confidence cases to a person, and record the outcome."},{"l":"B","t":"Draft a reply first, classify the ticket from the draft, and send every reply to the customer without a review step."},{"l":"C","t":"Route the ticket by the order in which it arrived, classify it after it is resolved, and report the result monthly."},{"l":"D","t":"Classify the ticket, resolve it autonomously with unrestricted tools, and review a random sample of the resolved outcomes once every quarter."}],"a":["A"],"r":"The reference flow is ingest, classify and extract, route, draft, human review on low confidence, and an outcome record that feeds evaluation. Drafting before classifying, or skipping review and feedback, breaks the loop.","x":["Correct. Ingest, classify and extract, route, draft, human review on low confidence and an outcome record that feeds evaluation is the reference flow.","Incorrect. Drafting before classifying and sending every reply without review breaks the flow and removes the safeguard.","Incorrect. Routing by arrival order and classifying after resolution defeats triage, and monthly reporting is too slow.","Incorrect. Autonomous resolution with unrestricted tools and a quarterly sample review leaves errors undetected for months."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design end-to-end architectures (input → processing → output → feedback loops)"},
{"id":"P126","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A data-engineering team wants Claude to refactor pipeline code in their repositories, run the tests, and propose changes for review. Which entry point fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A claude.ai Project where engineers paste in code and copy the results back by hand."},{"l":"B","t":"A custom chat interface built on the Messages API, with no access to the repositories."},{"l":"C","t":"Claude Code, which works inside the repository with file, shell, and test access under permission rules."},{"l":"D","t":"An MCP server that exposes the team's documentation, with no ability to change files."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Claude Code is built for work inside a repository with file, shell, and test access under permission boundaries. Copy-paste chat, an API with no repository access, and a read-only documentation server do not do the job.","x":["Incorrect. Pasting code into a Project and copying results back is manual and gives no ability to run the tests.","Incorrect. A chat interface with no repository access cannot refactor the pipelines or run the tests.","Correct. Claude Code works inside the repository with file, shell and test access under permission rules, and proposes changes for review.","Incorrect. A read-only documentation server cannot change files, so it cannot perform the refactor."],"df":"medium","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P127","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A software vendor wants a Claude assistant inside its own product, with its own login, its own interface, and per-customer usage metering. Which entry point fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give every customer a seat on a claude.ai Team plan and ask them to use the assistant there."},{"l":"B","t":"Run Claude Code on the vendor's own servers and let each customer's users connect to it directly, since it already provides an agent loop and an interface."},{"l":"C","t":"Use an MCP server alone, because it provides the interface that customers will see."},{"l":"D","t":"Build on the API (or the Agent SDK for an agent loop) so that the vendor controls authentication, the interface, and metering."}],"a":["D"],"r":"A vendor embedding Claude in its own product needs control of authentication, interface, and metering, which the API (or Agent SDK for an agent loop) provides. claude.ai seats and Claude Code are not multi-tenant backends.","x":["Incorrect. claude.ai seats are an end-user product and do not give the vendor its own login, interface or metering.","Incorrect. Claude Code is a developer tool, not a multi-tenant backend for customers.","Incorrect. An MCP server is not an interface for customers. It exposes tools and data to a client.","Correct. Building on the API, or the Agent SDK for an agent loop, lets the vendor control authentication, the interface and metering."],"df":"medium","ob":"Translate business problems into Claude-based solutions"},
{"id":"P128","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"An assistant emails policy explanations directly to customers with no review. It occasionally misstates the policy. Which redesign best reduces the risk without removing the automation?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Validate each draft's claims against the policy source and require human approval for replies the validation cannot confirm."},{"l":"B","t":"Tell the model in the prompt to be especially careful with policy statements and to double-check its work."},{"l":"C","t":"Add a disclaimer to every email saying that the content was generated by AI and may contain errors, so that customers know to check the policy themselves."},{"l":"D","t":"Send all replies to a human for approval, regardless of risk, so that nothing leaves the building unchecked."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Match control to risk: validate claims against the policy source and send only unconfirmed replies to a person. Prompting for care and disclaimers do not prevent errors, and approving everything removes the automation benefit.","x":["Correct. Validate the draft's claims against the policy source and send only unconfirmed replies to a person. This lowers the risk and keeps most of the automation.","Incorrect. Telling the model to be careful does not check anything, and misstatements can still reach customers.","Incorrect. A disclaimer does not prevent wrong policy statements and puts the checking on the customer.","Incorrect. Sending every reply to a person is safe but removes the automation, which the question asks to keep."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design end-to-end architectures (input → processing → output → feedback loops)"},
{"id":"P131","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A three-stage chain extracts data, normalises it, and drafts a summary. Each stage looks acceptable when tested alone, yet about one in eight final outputs is wrong. What is the best change?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Replace the three stages with one much longer prompt and drop the intermediate outputs altogether."},{"l":"B","t":"Increase the temperature on the middle stage so that it can correct upstream mistakes through variety."},{"l":"C","t":"Retry the whole chain three times and take the majority answer without examining any intermediate output."},{"l":"D","t":"Add validation gates between stages so that an error is caught where it occurs rather than passed on."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Chained stages compound errors, so check at the boundaries: validate each stage's output so that errors are caught where they occur. Merging stages or retrying blindly leaves the cause in place.","x":["Incorrect. One much longer prompt drops the intermediate outputs, so there is nothing to check and errors are harder to locate.","Incorrect. Higher temperature adds randomness and does not make a stage reliably fix upstream errors.","Incorrect. Retrying and voting without looking at intermediate outputs does not find where the error arises, and errors that are consistent will win the vote.","Correct. Validation gates between stages catch an error where it occurs, so it is not passed on and compounded."],"df":"medium","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P133","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A support agent cannot resolve a case after several attempts. What is the best escalation design?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Hand off to a person with a summary of the case, the steps already tried, and the customer's stated goal."},{"l":"B","t":"Tell the customer to start a new conversation, so that the next attempt begins from a clean slate."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep retrying silently with small variations until the agent produces some answer, however long that takes, so that no case is ever handed off."},{"l":"D","t":"Close the case with a standard apology message and invite the customer to try again later."}],"a":["A"],"r":"A good handoff carries the case summary, what was tried, and the customer's goal so the person does not start over. Silent retries and closing the case fail the customer.","x":["Correct. A handoff with a case summary, the steps tried and the customer's goal lets the person continue without making the customer start over.","Incorrect. Sending the customer back to start a new conversation loses the context and frustrates them.","Incorrect. Endless silent retries delay the customer and leave an unresolved case with no human involved.","Incorrect. Closing the case with an apology abandons the customer without resolving the issue."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design end-to-end architectures (input → processing → output → feedback loops)"},
{"id":"P135","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A workflow times out after calling a CRM-update tool and is retried by the orchestrator. Afterwards the CRM contains duplicate records. Which design change prevents this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Lower the timeout so that the call fails sooner and the retry begins before the first call has finished."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove retries from the workflow so that a failed step is never attempted twice under any circumstances."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the model to check whether a record exists before it calls the update tool each time."},{"l":"D","t":"Make side-effecting calls idempotent with a request key, so that a repeated call does not create a second record."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Retries after a timeout are only safe if side effects are idempotent. A request key lets the system recognise a repeated call and avoid creating a second record.","x":["Incorrect. A shorter timeout starts the retry before the first call finishes, which makes duplicates more likely.","Incorrect. Removing all retries makes the workflow fragile to ordinary transient failures.","Incorrect. A model check for an existing record is not reliable and leaves a race between the check and the update.","Correct. Idempotent side-effecting calls with a request key let a repeated call be recognised so it creates no second record."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design multi-agent systems and orchestration strategies"},
{"id":"P137","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A sponsor asks for one chatbot that handles twelve unrelated jobs, from payroll questions to IT resets. What is the best architectural recommendation?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"One assistant with one very large prompt that contains the instructions for all twelve jobs."},{"l":"B","t":"Twelve separate chatbots, each with its own login and interface, so that nothing is shared between them."},{"l":"C","t":"A front-door router that sends each request to a focused handler with its own prompt, tools, and limits."},{"l":"D","t":"An autonomous agent that holds every tool in the company and works out for itself what to do."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Decompose by job: a router in front of focused handlers, each with its own prompt, tools, and limits. One giant prompt blurs the jobs, twelve silos duplicate effort, and an everything-agent maximises risk.","x":["Incorrect. One very large prompt for twelve unrelated jobs blurs the instructions and makes behaviour hard to test.","Incorrect. Twelve separate chatbots with separate logins duplicate effort and give users a poor experience.","Correct. A front-door router sends each request to a focused handler with its own prompt, tools and limits.","Incorrect. An agent holding every company tool maximises risk and gives unpredictable behaviour."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply decomposition techniques for complex problem solving"},
{"id":"P138","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"Which task in a claims system is the best fit for Claude to do directly?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Computing the exact payout from policy terms and deductible tables."},{"l":"B","t":"Summarising an adjuster's free-text notes into a short, structured case summary."},{"l":"C","t":"Deciding on its own whether a claim should be paid and releasing the funds."},{"l":"D","t":"Storing the system of record for each customer's policy and claim history."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Summarising and structuring free text is a core language strength. Exact payout arithmetic and a system of record belong to deterministic systems, and releasing funds is a decision that needs authorisation.","x":["Incorrect. Exact payout arithmetic from policy terms and deductible tables is a task for deterministic code.","Correct. Summarising free-text notes into a short structured summary is a core language strength, and the output is reviewed by the adjuster.","Incorrect. Deciding on its own to pay and release funds is a decision that needs authorisation and a person or controlled process.","Incorrect. A system of record for policy and claim history belongs in a database, not in a model."],"df":"easy","ob":"Apply decomposition techniques for complex problem solving"},
{"id":"P141","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO situations favour deterministic routing in code over a Claude classifier? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The category must be inferred from the tone of a free-text message."},{"l":"B","t":"The route is fixed by a field already present on the request, such as account tier or channel."},{"l":"C","t":"The categories overlap and need subtle human-like judgement to separate."},{"l":"D","t":"The decision must be exact, repeatable, and easy to audit."},{"l":"E","t":"The incoming messages arrive in many languages and writing styles."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Use code when the route is determined by a field or when the decision must be exact and auditable. Tone, overlapping categories, and varied languages are where a model classifier adds value.","x":["Incorrect. Inferring a category from tone is what a model classifier does well, so it does not favour code.","Correct. When a field already on the request decides the route, such as account tier or channel, plain code is simpler and exact.","Incorrect. Overlapping categories that need subtle judgement are where a model classifier adds value.","Correct. Decisions that must be exact, repeatable and auditable fit deterministic code.","Incorrect. Many languages and writing styles are handled well by a model, and rules would struggle."],"df":"medium","ob":"Select appropriate architectural patterns (workflow, agentic, augmented LLM)"},
{"id":"P20","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Leadership asks the team to run every call on the most capable model tier 'to be safe'. No evaluation set exists yet. What should the architect recommend?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Choose the smallest tier to guarantee low spend, and add a larger one only if complaints begin to arrive."},{"l":"B","t":"Adopt the largest tier for every call now, since accuracy matters most, and revisit cost once the first invoice arrives."},{"l":"C","t":"Default to Sonnet, build an eval set with a pre-agreed threshold, and change tiers only if the eval demands it."},{"l":"D","t":"Split traffic randomly across all tiers and keep whichever one draws the fewest complaints over a month."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Not deciding on a tier is itself a decision that defaults to the most expensive option. Sonnet is the balanced default; moves up or down are releases gated by an eval with a threshold set in advance.","x":["Incorrect. Choosing the smallest tier by default and waiting for complaints makes users the test and may hurt quality.","Incorrect. Defaulting to the largest tier for every call is the expensive default that leadership asked for without evidence.","Correct. Default to Sonnet, build an eval with a threshold agreed in advance, and change tiers only if the eval demands it.","Incorrect. Random traffic splits judged by complaints are not a controlled evaluation, and complaints are a poor signal."],"df":"easy","ob":"Select appropriate Claude models based on trade-offs"},
{"id":"P21","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A contract-review assistant sends a 12,000-token playbook and instruction block on every call, followed by a different clause each time. Calls arrive every few seconds. Which prompt layout best reduces cost and latency?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Summarise the playbook down to 500 tokens so that caching becomes unnecessary and the prompt stays short."},{"l":"B","t":"Place the cache breakpoint after the clause so that the entire prompt, including the clause, is cached."},{"l":"C","t":"Instructions and playbook first, cache breakpoint after them, and the clause appended after the breakpoint."},{"l":"D","t":"Put the clause first so the model reads the specific task before the long background, and cache whatever follows."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Caching works on a stable prefix: static content first, dynamic content after the breakpoint. Putting the changing clause first or inside the cached span defeats reuse, and summarising the playbook discards required content.","x":["Incorrect. Shrinking the playbook to 500 tokens discards required content, and caching is still worthwhile for a stable prefix.","Incorrect. A breakpoint after the clause would cache a prefix that changes on every call, so it would never be reused.","Correct. Put the stable instructions and playbook first, set the breakpoint after them, and append the changing clause after it.","Incorrect. Putting the changing clause first changes the prefix each call, so nothing after it can be a cache hit."],"df":"medium","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P23","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"After a release, cache hit rate falls from about 85% to nearly zero and cost rises, although traffic and model are unchanged. The diff shows the current date and time were added as the first line of the system prompt. What explains this and what is the fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The system prompt became too long; delete half of the guardrails so that the prompt fits in the cache."},{"l":"B","t":"The cache entries expired; increase `max_tokens` so that each generated response is cached for longer."},{"l":"C","t":"The cached prefix now differs on every request; move the timestamp after the cache breakpoint."},{"l":"D","t":"The model tier was silently changed; revert to the previous model version and re-enable caching."}],"a":["C"],"r":"A cache hit requires an identical prefix. A request-specific value at the top changes the prefix every time. Keep the fixed part genuinely fixed and put variable content after the breakpoint.","x":["Incorrect. Deleting guardrails weakens the system and does not address why the prefix stopped matching.","Incorrect. Cache entries do not depend on max_tokens, and expiry would not drop the hit rate to zero right after this change.","Correct. The timestamp at the top changes the prefix every request, so nothing matches. Move it after the cache breakpoint.","Incorrect. The model is unchanged, as the question states, so reverting a model version would not help."],"df":"hard","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P24","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A request completes, but the output is cut off mid-sentence and the response carries the stop reason `model_context_window_exceeded`. What does this indicate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The prompt cache expired in the middle of generation, so the model lost its prefix and stopped."},{"l":"B","t":"Generation hit the context window ceiling after the prompt fit; track usage and token counts and compact context."},{"l":"C","t":"The request was rejected before generation began because the prompt exceeded the token limit (400 `invalid_request_error`)."},{"l":"D","t":"The raw request body exceeded the API's byte limit, so the request was refused as too large (413 `request_too_large`)."}],"a":["B"],"r":"There are two distinct edge errors: an oversized request is rejected before generation (400 or 413), whereas a request that fits but whose generation hits the window ends with a truncated response and this stop reason. Check usage and token counting before sending.","x":["Incorrect. This stop reason is about the context window ceiling. It has nothing to do with the prompt cache expiring.","Correct. The prompt fit, but generation ran into the context window ceiling and the output was cut off. Track usage and token counts and compact the context.","Incorrect. A prompt that exceeds the token limit is rejected before generation with a 400 error. Here generation had already started and the output was truncated.","Incorrect. A request body over the byte limit is refused with a 413 before generation. This response was truncated mid-sentence, so the request was accepted."],"df":"hard","ob":"Optimize context windows and manage token usage"},
{"id":"P25","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A ticket-severity rubric has subtle boundaries, such as 'degraded but usable' versus 'partially unavailable'. Instruction-only prompts give inconsistent labels. Which technique is the best next step?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Reduce the rubric to two categories and keep the instructions otherwise unchanged."},{"l":"B","t":"Rewrite the zero-shot prompt with stronger wording such as 'be consistent' and 'follow the rubric exactly'."},{"l":"C","t":"Few-shot examples showing representative inputs and the correct labels, especially near the boundaries."},{"l":"D","t":"Increase `max_tokens` so that the model has more room to explain each severity decision it makes."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Few-shot fits tasks where the desired judgment is easier to show than to describe. Stronger wording and larger output limits do not clarify an ambiguous boundary.","x":["Incorrect. Reducing to two categories loses the distinction the business needs, and does not fix the labelling at the boundaries.","Incorrect. Stronger wording does not clarify a subtle boundary, so labels stay inconsistent.","Correct. Few-shot examples show the judgement, especially near the boundaries, which is easier to demonstrate than describe.","Incorrect. A larger output limit does not make the rubric clearer."],"df":"easy","ob":"Apply prompt engineering techniques (zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought)"},
{"id":"P26","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"After moving to a newer, more capable model, a team keeps a 12-example few-shot block and detailed step-by-step instructions written for the older model. What is the best practice?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Re-run the eval set and trim scaffolding only where the eval shows quality holds."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove all examples and instructions immediately, because newer models never need any scaffolding."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep everything unchanged, because a prompt behaves identically on every model tier and generation."},{"l":"D","t":"Add more examples to the prompt, since more examples always help a more capable model."}],"a":["A"],"r":"The same prompt does not behave identically across models; a more capable model may need less scaffolding, but that is an empirical question answered by the eval, not an assumption.","x":["Correct. Whether a newer model needs less scaffolding is an empirical question. Re-run the eval and trim only where quality holds.","Incorrect. Newer models do not never need scaffolding, and removing everything at once without an eval risks a silent quality drop.","Incorrect. A prompt does not behave identically across models, so carrying it over unchanged without testing is an assumption.","Incorrect. More examples do not always help a more capable model, and they can add cost and noise. The eval should decide."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply prompt engineering techniques (zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought)"},
{"id":"P29","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Five product teams need the same incident-postmortem procedure. It must be versioned, approved by compliance, and roll-back-able. Which reuse mechanism fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Embed it in each team's system prompt and let each team evolve its own copy over time."},{"l":"B","t":"Package it as a Skill: a versioned, governed unit with instructions and optional scripts."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep it in the lead engineer's personal prompt library and share it on request."},{"l":"D","t":"Share a wiki page of prompt fragments that each team copies into its own code and adapts."}],"a":["B"],"r":"When a stable procedure must be distributed across teams with versioning, approval, and rollback, a Skill fits. Copied prompts drift into many slightly different versions with quietly different behaviour.","x":["Incorrect. Separate copies in each team's system prompt drift into different versions with different behaviour, and none is versioned or approved centrally.","Correct. A Skill is a versioned, governed unit with instructions and optional scripts. It can be approved by compliance, distributed to every team and rolled back.","Incorrect. One engineer's personal library shared on request has no versioning, approval or rollback, and depends on one person.","Incorrect. Copied wiki fragments get adapted separately in each team's code, so they drift and cannot be governed centrally."],"df":"medium","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P30","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO situations favour a monolithic context strategy over progressive loading? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"An agent loop running 80 turns where each step depends mainly on recent state."},{"l":"B","t":"A knowledge base that changes several times a day."},{"l":"C","t":"A bounded task with predictable input size where retrieval latency is not worth paying."},{"l":"D","t":"A stable instruction prefix reused on every call that benefits from prompt caching."},{"l":"E","t":"A multi-turn dialogue that accumulates tool outputs turn after turn."}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Monolithic earns its place for bounded, predictable tasks and stable cacheable prefixes. Long loops and accumulating conversations are where cost and attention degrade, and fast-changing sources are better retrieved.","x":["Incorrect. In a long loop each step depends mainly on recent state, so carrying everything is costly and degrades attention. Progressive loading suits it better.","Incorrect. A knowledge base that changes several times a day is better retrieved on demand, because a monolithic copy goes stale.","Correct. For a bounded task with a predictable input size, retrieval latency is not worth paying. Loading everything up front is simpler.","Correct. A stable instruction prefix reused on every call is what prompt caching rewards, which makes a monolithic prefix efficient.","Incorrect. Accumulating tool outputs turn after turn is where cost and attention degrade, so progressive loading or compaction fits better."],"df":"medium","ob":"Optimize context windows and manage token usage"},
{"id":"P149","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A prompt contains instructions, a policy document, and a customer email in one block of plain text. The model sometimes treats sentences in the email as instructions. Which change helps most?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Put the whole prompt in capital letters so that the instructions stand out from the other content."},{"l":"B","t":"Wrap each part in clearly named tags, such as policy and email tags, and refer to them by name in the instructions."},{"l":"C","t":"Move the customer email into the system prompt so that the model gives it more weight and reads it before the instructions and the policy."},{"l":"D","t":"Remove the policy document entirely so that there is less text for the model to confuse with instructions."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Named tags separate instructions from data and let instructions refer to each part precisely. Capitals, moving data into the system prompt, or deleting needed content do not create that separation.","x":["Incorrect. Capital letters do not separate instructions from data, and the model can still treat email sentences as instructions.","Correct. Named tags separate each part, and instructions can refer to them by name, for example 'treat the email content as data'.","Incorrect. Moving the customer's text into the system prompt gives untrusted content more authority, which increases the risk.","Incorrect. Removing the policy document takes away content the task needs, and does not fix the confusion about the email."],"df":"easy","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P151","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"What is the best division of content between the system prompt and the user turn in a support assistant?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The system prompt holds the role, stable rules, and output format; the user turn holds the request-specific content."},{"l":"B","t":"The system prompt holds the customer's message; the user turn holds the rules, so that the rules are read last."},{"l":"C","t":"Everything goes in the user turn, because the system prompt is ignored by current models."},{"l":"D","t":"Everything goes in the system prompt, including each customer's message, which is rebuilt from scratch for every single request."}],"a":["A"],"r":"The system prompt carries what is stable: role, rules, and format. The user turn carries what changes per request. That split is clearer for the model and keeps the stable part cacheable.","x":["Correct. The system prompt carries what is stable, such as role, rules and format, and the user turn carries the content that changes per request.","Incorrect. This reverses the split. Putting the customer's message in the system prompt mixes changing content with stable rules.","Incorrect. System prompts are not ignored by current models, and they are the right place for stable rules.","Incorrect. Putting each customer's message into the system prompt rebuilds it every request and loses the stable, cacheable prefix."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P153","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A team asks the model to reason step by step before answering, and accuracy improves. However, the reasoning now appears in the customer-facing reply. What is the best fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Remove the step-by-step instruction altogether, accepting the lower accuracy as the price of keeping customer replies short and clean."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask the model to keep its reasoning very short so that customers do not mind reading it."},{"l":"C","t":"Show the reasoning to customers, since it makes the answer look more trustworthy."},{"l":"D","t":"Ask for reasoning in one tagged section and the answer in another, and show only the answer; or use extended thinking."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Keep the accuracy gain and separate the reasoning from the answer: tagged sections that the application strips, or extended thinking, whose reasoning is returned in its own block.","x":["Incorrect. Removing the instruction throws away the accuracy gain when the problem is only where the reasoning is shown.","Incorrect. Shorter reasoning is still reasoning shown to customers, and it may reduce the accuracy benefit.","Incorrect. Customers do not need the working, and visible reasoning can contain errors or internal details.","Correct. Ask for reasoning in one tagged section and the answer in another, and show only the answer, or use extended thinking, whose reasoning arrives in its own block."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply prompt engineering techniques (zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought)"},
{"id":"P154","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A prompt says, 'Do not use markdown, bullet points, or headers.' The model still sometimes produces lists. Which revision is most likely to help?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Repeat the prohibition three times at different points in the prompt so that it is harder to miss."},{"l":"B","t":"Say what to do instead: 'Write in flowing prose paragraphs', and make the prompt itself prose-styled."},{"l":"C","t":"Add a line saying that using markdown will result in a penalty for the model."},{"l":"D","t":"Raise the temperature so that the model moves away from its habit of producing lists."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Positive instructions, saying what to do, work better than prohibitions, and the prompt's own style influences the output's style. Repetition, threats, and temperature changes do not address the cause.","x":["Incorrect. Repeating a prohibition does not tell the model what to do instead and often does not change behaviour.","Correct. Say what to do, 'Write in flowing prose paragraphs', and write the prompt itself in prose, because the style of the prompt influences the output.","Incorrect. Threatening a penalty does not give the model a clear alternative and does not change how it writes.","Incorrect. Temperature does not address the formatting habit."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P158","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A request caches a prefix made of tool definitions and a system prompt, with a breakpoint after the system prompt. Which TWO changes will cause the cached prefix to miss? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Editing the description of one tool definition."},{"l":"B","t":"Changing the user's question that follows the breakpoint."},{"l":"C","t":"Reordering two sections inside the system prompt."},{"l":"D","t":"Appending a new conversation turn after the breakpoint."},{"l":"E","t":"Sending the next request with a different customer's message."}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"A cache hit needs an identical prefix up to the breakpoint. Editing a tool definition or reordering the system prompt changes that prefix. Changes after the breakpoint do not affect the cached part.","x":["Correct. Editing a tool definition changes the cached prefix, so the cache misses.","Incorrect. The user's question follows the breakpoint, so changing it does not affect the cached prefix.","Correct. Reordering sections inside the system prompt changes the prefix, so the cache misses.","Incorrect. A turn appended after the breakpoint is outside the cached prefix.","Incorrect. A different customer message falls after the breakpoint, so the cached prefix is still a hit."],"df":"hard","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P164","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A pipeline asks the model to 'return JSON' and parses the result. Roughly one response in fifty is malformed or missing fields. Which approach makes output structure most reliable?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask the model more firmly to return valid JSON, and add 'IMPORTANT' before the instruction."},{"l":"B","t":"Parse the response with a lenient parser that guesses at any missing fields."},{"l":"C","t":"Define the output with a JSON schema through tool use or structured outputs, and validate before use."},{"l":"D","t":"Lower `max_tokens` so that the model has less room to produce malformed text."}],"a":["C"],"r":"A schema enforced through tool use or structured outputs, plus validation, makes structure reliable. Firmer wording and lenient parsing leave the failure in place or hide it.","x":["Incorrect. A firmer instruction and capital letters do not guarantee valid JSON, so some responses will still fail.","Incorrect. A lenient parser that guesses missing fields hides errors and puts invented data into the pipeline.","Correct. A JSON schema through tool use or structured outputs, plus validation before use, makes structure reliable.","Incorrect. A lower token limit can truncate the output and make malformed JSON more likely."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P168","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A team builds prompts from shared modules: a tone module, a safety module, and task-specific modules. What is a key benefit, and a risk to manage?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"**Benefit:** modules remove the need for evaluation. Risk: modules make prompts longer."},{"l":"B","t":"**Benefit:** each team can edit shared modules freely. Risk: none, as modules are independent."},{"l":"C","t":"**Benefit:** a shared rule changes in one place. Risk: a module change affects every prompt using it and can break caching."},{"l":"D","t":"**Benefit:** modules guarantee identical behaviour on every model and tier. Risk: modules cannot be placed under version control."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Modules let a shared rule change once, but that change ripples into every prompt that uses it and can invalidate cached prefixes. Version and eval module changes accordingly.","x":["Incorrect. Modules do not remove the need for evaluation. Changes to them still need testing.","Incorrect. Shared modules are not risk-free, because editing one affects every prompt that uses it.","Correct. A shared rule changes in one place, but a module change affects every prompt that uses it and can break caching, so version and eval it.","Incorrect. Modules do not guarantee identical behaviour across models, and they can be version-controlled."],"df":"medium","ob":"Implement prompt reuse strategies (caching, modular prompts, Skills)"},
{"id":"P170","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"An 8,000-token system prompt has grown over a year. Critical rules buried in the middle are sometimes missed. What is the best response?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Restructure with clear sections, put critical constraints prominently, remove redundant text, and confirm with the eval."},{"l":"B","t":"Add each critical rule again at the end of the prompt, leaving the rest unchanged."},{"l":"C","t":"Move the whole prompt into the user turn so that the model reads it more closely."},{"l":"D","t":"Switch to a larger model so that it reads the full prompt more carefully and is less likely to skip the rules buried in the middle."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Long prompts accumulate redundancy that hides key rules. Restructure, make critical constraints prominent, prune, and confirm with the eval.","x":["Correct. Restructure into clear sections, make the critical constraints prominent, remove redundant text and confirm with the eval.","Incorrect. Repeating each rule at the end adds more text and leaves the buried structure unchanged.","Incorrect. Moving the whole prompt to the user turn does not fix the buried rules and loses the stable system prompt.","Incorrect. A larger model does not fix an overgrown prompt, and critical rules can still be missed."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design system prompts, templates, and guardrails"},
{"id":"P36","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A Claude-powered analytics assistant queries the data warehouse through a shared service account with broad read access. A junior analyst obtained figures from a restricted region. What is the root cause and fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The model is leaking restricted data; add a system-prompt line forbidding it from mentioning restricted regions."},{"l":"B","t":"The shared service account is too slow under load; add a caching layer in front of the warehouse queries."},{"l":"C","t":"Add output screening that blocks any response containing the word 'restricted' before it is shown."},{"l":"D","t":"Authorization was bypassed; enforce the user's permissions at the data or retrieval layer before Claude sees content."}],"a":["D"],"r":"A Claude layer that bypasses the underlying authorization model gives users data through a path never designed to enforce policy. Fix it where access is decided, not in prompt wording.","x":["Incorrect. A prompt line is not an access control, and the model can still be steered into returning the data.","Incorrect. The issue is access, not speed, so a caching layer does not help and could even spread restricted data.","Incorrect. Blocking the word 'restricted' does not stop restricted figures being shown and is easy to evade.","Correct. Authorization was bypassed. Enforce the user's permissions at the data or retrieval layer before Claude sees the content."],"df":"hard","ob":"Analyze authentication and authorization requirements to identify security gaps"},
{"id":"P38","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A chat assistant has a p95 latency target of 3 seconds. Enabling extended thinking on every request improves accuracy on complex questions but pushes p95 to 6 seconds. What is the best configuration decision?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise the p95 target to 6 seconds so that the SLA is met with the current configuration."},{"l":"B","t":"Keep extended thinking on for every request, because accuracy always outranks latency in a chat product."},{"l":"C","t":"Use extended thinking only for request classes where the eval shows the gain justifies the latency."},{"l":"D","t":"Disable extended thinking everywhere without testing, so that p95 returns to the target immediately."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Accuracy and latency trade off and the decision should be justified with evidence per request class. Moving the SLA to fit the configuration inverts the logic: latency targets come from user-experience expectations.","x":["Incorrect. Moving the target to fit the configuration inverts the logic, since latency targets come from user expectations.","Incorrect. Accuracy does not always outrank latency in a chat product, and a p95 of 6 seconds breaks the target.","Correct. Use extended thinking only for the request classes where the eval shows the gain is worth the latency.","Incorrect. Disabling it everywhere without testing gives up the accuracy gain on complex questions."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P39","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A retailer wants to re-summarise 80,000 archived product reviews to populate a new catalogue. The job can run overnight and the results are needed the next morning. Which TWO considerations are correct? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The batch discount and the maximum requests per batch should be confirmed against current published pricing before the cost model is fixed."},{"l":"B","t":"Batch processing lowers the quality of the output in exchange for the discount that it provides."},{"l":"C","t":"An overnight job cannot use prompt caching, so the stable instruction block has to be removed from each request."},{"l":"D","t":"The asynchronous deadline makes the Batch API a candidate for a substantial reduction in cost."},{"l":"E","t":"Batch requests need a larger context window than standard calls, so long reviews have to be split first."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Where the SLA permits asynchronous processing, the Batch API is a cost alternative worth modelling, with its discount rate and batch size limit verified against current pricing rather than assumed. Batch does not change output quality or the context window, and call volume remains an input to the cost model.","x":["Correct. The batch discount and the batch size limits should be checked against current published pricing before the cost model is fixed.","Incorrect. Batch processing does not lower output quality. The trade-off is that results are asynchronous.","Incorrect. Prompt caching can still be used with an overnight job, so the stable block does not need to be removed.","Correct. A job that can wait overnight makes the Batch API a candidate for a substantial cost reduction.","Incorrect. Batch requests do not need a larger context window than standard calls."],"df":"hard","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P41","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Support agents paste customers' national ID numbers into messages sent to Claude, and the application's request logs now hold them in plaintext. What is the best architectural change?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask Claude in the system prompt to ignore national ID numbers and never to repeat them in its replies."},{"l":"B","t":"Encrypt the log disk and keep the plaintext ID numbers in the prompts so that agents work as before."},{"l":"C","t":"Rotate the application's log files more frequently so that the plaintext IDs are retained for less time."},{"l":"D","t":"Redact or tokenise sensitive fields server-side before the API call and before logging; pass reference IDs."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Anything placed in the request becomes part of the API call and any application-layer logging. Decide which fields are necessary in the context window, and redact before the call; retroactive cleanup of logs is the most expensive fix.","x":["Incorrect. An instruction to ignore the numbers does not remove them. They are still sent in the request and still end up in the logs.","Incorrect. Encrypting the disk protects the storage but leaves the plaintext IDs in the prompts and the API call.","Incorrect. Rotating logs more often shortens retention but leaves the IDs in the logs in the meantime, and still sends them to the model.","Correct. Redact or tokenise sensitive fields server-side before the API call and before logging, and pass reference IDs. The data never enters the request or the logs."],"df":"medium","ob":"Analyze authentication and authorization requirements to identify security gaps"},
{"id":"P42","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A RAG system over 400-page regulatory manuals returns clauses stripped of their headings and parent sections, leading to misinterpretation. Which chunking approach best fits this corpus?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Single-sentence chunks, so that each retrieved unit is as precise and as small as possible."},{"l":"B","t":"Random sampling of manual pages at query time, to avoid bias toward any one section."},{"l":"C","t":"Fixed-size chunks with a larger overlap so that neighbouring text appears in more than one chunk."},{"l":"D","t":"Hierarchical chunking that preserves sections and subsections and retrieves at the right level."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Chunking is chosen by the structure of the source. Structured documents where section context carries meaning call for hierarchical chunking; fixed-size spans cut across that structure.","x":["Incorrect. Single-sentence chunks remove the headings and parent sections that give clauses their meaning.","Incorrect. Random page sampling at query time does not retrieve the relevant clauses and loses structure.","Incorrect. More overlap in fixed-size chunks still cuts across section boundaries and does not preserve headings or parents.","Correct. Hierarchical chunking preserves sections and subsections and retrieves at the right level, which suits structured manuals."],"df":"easy","ob":"Design a RAG pipeline with appropriate chunking and indexing strategies"},
{"id":"P43","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Users query a parts catalog with codes such as 'HX-4471-B' and also with phrases like 'the connector that keeps rattling'. Dense retrieval misses exact-code queries. Which indexing strategy fits?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Dense-only retrieval with a larger embedding model, which will learn to match part codes exactly."},{"l":"B","t":"Dense-only retrieval over 50-token fixed chunks, so that each code sits in a very small chunk."},{"l":"C","t":"Hybrid retrieval: dense embeddings for phrasing plus sparse keyword matching (BM25) for exact codes."},{"l":"D","t":"Sparse-only keyword retrieval, dropping semantic search because it cannot match part codes."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Indexing is chosen by the query pattern. Mixed queries, the common production case, need both semantic and exact-term matching, which hybrid combines.","x":["Incorrect. A larger embedding model will not reliably match exact part codes, which dense retrieval handles poorly.","Incorrect. Small chunks do not make dense vectors match an exact code. They also lose context.","Correct. Hybrid retrieval combines dense embeddings for phrases with keyword matching such as BM25 for exact codes.","Incorrect. Sparse-only retrieval handles codes but fails on phrases like 'the connector that keeps rattling'."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply retrieval strategies matched to data shape and query pattern"},
{"id":"P46","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A company wants an assistant inside its multi-tenant web app that works over multiple turns with tools while the application controls the surrounding workflow. Which build-time choice fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A claude.ai Team plan with shared Projects, giving every customer a seat in the organisation."},{"l":"B","t":"The Claude Agent SDK, which provides a managed agent loop to embed inside the application."},{"l":"C","t":"Claude Code as the backend for all customers, since it already runs the agent loop for developers."},{"l":"D","t":"A single Messages API call per user message with no tools, orchestrated entirely by the front end."}],"a":["B"],"r":"The Agent SDK exists for embedding a multi-turn agent loop inside a partner's own product. Claude Code is a developer tool, not a multi-tenant backend, and claude.ai is an end-user product.","x":["Incorrect. claude.ai Team is an end-user product with seats, not a way to build an assistant inside your own multi-tenant application.","Correct. The Agent SDK provides a managed multi-turn agent loop with tools to embed inside your own application, while the application controls the workflow.","Incorrect. Claude Code is a developer tool, not a multi-tenant backend for customers.","Incorrect. A single call per message with no tools does not give a multi-turn assistant with tools, and pushes orchestration to the front end."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P49","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A manufacturer runs its ML platform, billing, and audit on Google Cloud and wants one billing and audit entry point across foundation models for a report-generation application. Which delivery route and deciding factor fit best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Vertex AI, because billing, authentication, and audit already sit in the firm's GCP environment."},{"l":"B","t":"claude.ai Team, because it needs no integration work and can be rolled out immediately."},{"l":"C","t":"AWS Bedrock, because it is the most widely used route and has the broadest customer base."},{"l":"D","t":"The direct Anthropic API, because it always has the newest features regardless of procurement."}],"a":["A"],"r":"The delivery route follows the partner's existing infrastructure and agreements. Vertex keeps identity, billing, and audit in the existing cloud, whereas Bedrock would cross clouds and claude.ai is an end-user product, not a programmatic route.","x":["Correct. The delivery route follows existing infrastructure. Vertex AI keeps billing, authentication and audit inside the firm's GCP environment.","Incorrect. claude.ai Team is an end-user product, not a programmatic route for a report-generation application, and it would not give one audit entry point across foundation models.","Incorrect. Popularity is not a deciding factor, and Bedrock would cross clouds for a firm whose billing and audit sit on Google Cloud.","Incorrect. Having the newest features does not outweigh procurement and audit needs, and the direct API would sit outside the firm's cloud billing and audit."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P51","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team proposes adding a second retrieval stage and a re-ranking model to lift answer quality in a RAG assistant. Which TWO consequences must be weighed before adopting it? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Each added stage adds retrieval latency to every request, which has to fit inside the response budget."},{"l":"B","t":"A longer retrieval pipeline allows live transactional state to be answered from the index."},{"l":"C","t":"Extra stages make the index immune to staleness when documents are added to or removed from the corpus."},{"l":"D","t":"There is more pipeline to keep correct as the corpus grows and changes, which is a recurring maintenance cost."},{"l":"E","t":"Re-ranking removes the need to track retrieval precision and recall as system metrics."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Every retrieval design trades off retrieval quality, latency, and maintenance. Extra stages can raise quality while adding per-request latency and more pipeline to keep correct. Retrieval precision and recall still need measuring, staleness still comes from indexing, and live state still belongs behind a tool call.","x":["Correct. Each added stage adds latency to every request, which has to fit within the response budget.","Incorrect. A longer retrieval pipeline does not make an index suitable for live transactional state, which belongs behind a tool call.","Incorrect. Extra stages do not remove staleness. That depends on how documents are indexed.","Correct. More pipeline means more to keep correct as the corpus grows and changes, which is a recurring maintenance cost.","Incorrect. Re-ranking does not remove the need to measure retrieval precision and recall."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design a RAG pipeline with appropriate chunking and indexing strategies"},
{"id":"P53","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A code-review subagent inside a repository agent currently inherits the main session's full tool set, including file writes and the deploy command. Review runs only need to read files and post comments. What is the appropriate configuration?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep the inherited tools and read the subagent's transcripts after each run so misuse can be caught later."},{"l":"B","t":"Give the subagent a restricted, read-only tool set scoped to the review task instead of inheriting the parent's tools."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep the inherited tools and add an instruction to the subagent's prompt telling it not to write files or deploy."},{"l":"D","t":"Remove the subagent and have the main session carry out reviews with the tools it already holds."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Subagents exist partly so bounded work can run with a restricted tool entry point and its own system prompt. Scoping the tools to what the task needs removes the capability rather than instructing or monitoring around it, which is least privilege applied to tools.","x":["Incorrect. Reading transcripts afterwards only detects misuse after the fact. The capability to write files and deploy is still there.","Correct. A restricted read-only tool set scoped to the review task removes the capability itself, which is least privilege applied to tools.","Incorrect. A prompt instruction is only a request. The subagent still holds write and deploy tools, so the control is not enforced.","Incorrect. Removing the subagent gives the main session even more tools, and loses the isolated, restricted entry point that subagents provide."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate tool/agent configuration for capability bloat"},
{"id":"P186","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Finance users ask questions such as 'Total spend by vendor in Q3 above ₹10 lakh.' The data sits in a structured database. Which integration fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Export the tables to text, chunk them, and answer through vector search."},{"l":"B","t":"Paste the entire database export into the prompt for each question."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the model to estimate the totals from a sample of rows."},{"l":"D","t":"Give the model a query tool over the database, with read-only access and validated queries."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Aggregations over structured data need a query engine, not similarity search. A read-only, validated query tool gives exact answers. Estimating from samples is not acceptable for finance.","x":["Incorrect. Vector search over chunked text returns similar passages, not exact totals, so it cannot do aggregation reliably.","Incorrect. Pasting the whole database export for each question is costly, may exceed the context window and does not compute sums reliably.","Incorrect. Estimating totals from a sample of rows gives approximate figures, which is not acceptable for finance.","Correct. A read-only query tool with validated queries lets the database compute exact aggregations and the model only builds and explains the query."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply retrieval strategies matched to data shape and query pattern"},
{"id":"P189","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An assistant must cite its sources, and auditors need to trust the citations. Which TWO design elements support this? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Pass each retrieved passage with a stable source identifier and require answers to cite by that identifier."},{"l":"B","t":"Ask the model to add citations from memory after it has written the answer, choosing the documents it thinks fit best."},{"l":"C","t":"Let the model invent plausible document titles where it cannot find one."},{"l":"D","t":"Remove citations from answers that are longer than a paragraph."},{"l":"E","t":"Check that each cited passage exists and contains the quoted text before showing the answer."}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"Trusted citations need stable source IDs passed with the passages and a check that each citation exists and contains the quoted text. Citations from memory are unverified.","x":["Correct. Passing each retrieved passage with a stable source identifier and requiring citation by that identifier ties each claim to a real source.","Incorrect. Citations added from memory after the answer is written are unverified and can be wrong.","Incorrect. Inventing plausible titles produces fake citations, which destroys trust.","Incorrect. Removing citations from longer answers hides sources exactly where they matter.","Correct. Checking that each cited passage exists and contains the quoted text before showing the answer makes the citations verifiable."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design a RAG pipeline with appropriate chunking and indexing strategies"},
{"id":"P190","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Employees ask questions in Tamil and Hindi, while the policy documents are in English. Retrieval quality is poor for non-English queries. What is the best approach?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask employees to write all queries in English."},{"l":"B","t":"Index the documents only by their titles and headings, so that the language of the query matters much less during retrieval."},{"l":"C","t":"Use a multilingual embedding model, or translate the query before retrieval, and evaluate per language."},{"l":"D","t":"Increase the number of retrieved passages for non-English queries only."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Cross-language retrieval needs multilingual embeddings or query translation, with quality evaluated per language. Forcing users into English shifts the problem to them.","x":["Incorrect. Forcing employees to write in English shifts the problem onto users and loses the benefit of the assistant.","Incorrect. Indexing only titles and headings throws away most of the content and does not fix cross-language matching.","Correct. A multilingual embedding model, or translating the query before retrieval, handles the language gap, and evaluation per language shows if it works.","Incorrect. Retrieving more passages for non-English queries adds noise and does not fix poor matching."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply retrieval strategies matched to data shape and query pattern"},
{"id":"P192","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"An MCP server needs to expose read-only reference material, such as a product catalogue, for the client to include as context. Which MCP primitive fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A tool, because every capability an MCP server offers must be a tool."},{"l":"B","t":"A resource, which exposes data the client can read into context."},{"l":"C","t":"A prompt, because prompts are the only way to pass data to the model."},{"l":"D","t":"A sampling request, because the server must ask the model for the catalogue."}],"a":["B"],"r":"MCP servers expose tools (actions), resources (data to read into context), and prompts (reusable templates). Read-only reference material fits a resource.","x":["Incorrect. MCP servers offer more than tools. Tools are for actions, and read-only data to include as context fits a resource.","Correct. A resource exposes data the client can read into context, which fits read-only reference material like a catalogue.","Incorrect. A prompt is a reusable template. It is not the way to expose a catalogue of data.","Incorrect. Sampling is how a server asks the client's model to generate text. It is not a way to expose reference data."],"df":"easy","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P195","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"An internal web application needs Claude to call one internal pricing service. No other client will use this tool. Which integration is the simplest adequate choice?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Build an MCP server for the pricing service, so that it can be reused straight away if other clients appear in the future."},{"l":"B","t":"Build an agent-to-agent interface so that the pricing service becomes its own agent."},{"l":"C","t":"Define the tool directly in the application's API requests and call the service from application code."},{"l":"D","t":"Run Claude Code on the server and let it call the pricing service through the shell."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Choose the lightest mechanism that meets the need. With one client and one service, a tool defined in the application's own requests is simplest. MCP's value is reuse across clients.","x":["Incorrect. MCP is valuable for reuse across clients, and building a server for a single client now is extra work with no present benefit.","Incorrect. Turning a pricing service into its own agent is unnecessary for a single lookup.","Correct. With one client and one service, define the tool directly in the application's API requests and call the service from application code.","Incorrect. Running Claude Code on the server to use the shell is heavy and unsuited to a simple application call."],"df":"hard","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P196","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A chat assistant takes eight seconds to produce a full answer. Users abandon the chat before it appears. Total time cannot be reduced much. What helps most?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise `max_tokens` so that the answer is complete when it arrives."},{"l":"B","t":"Stream the response so that users see text within the first second or two."},{"l":"C","t":"Show a spinner and send the full answer only when it is complete."},{"l":"D","t":"Cut the answer to one sentence for every question."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Streaming cuts time to first token, which is what users perceive. Total time is unchanged, but abandonment drops.","x":["Incorrect. Raising max_tokens affects the maximum length, not how fast the answer appears.","Correct. Streaming shows text within a second or two, which cuts the perceived wait even though the total time is similar.","Incorrect. A spinner with the answer sent only at the end leaves users waiting the full eight seconds.","Incorrect. One-sentence answers for every question remove value to fix a perception problem."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P197","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A request performs a policy lookup (1.5 s), a customer lookup (1.2 s), and then a model call (3 s). The two lookups are independent. The p95 target is 5 s and currently runs at 6.2 s. Which change targets the problem?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run the two independent lookups in parallel so that the slower one, not their sum, sets the wait."},{"l":"B","t":"Move to a larger model tier, on the basis that a more capable model completes its part of the request faster."},{"l":"C","t":"Add a second model call to check the first answer before replying."},{"l":"D","t":"Raise the p95 target to 7 s so that the current design passes."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Independent calls should run in parallel so the wait is set by the slowest, here about 1.5 s plus 3 s. Moving the target or adding calls does not fix the design.","x":["Correct. Running the two independent lookups in parallel means the slower one, not their sum, sets the wait.","Incorrect. A larger tier is usually slower and does not remove the sequential lookups.","Incorrect. A second model call adds latency, which makes the p95 worse.","Incorrect. Raising the target to pass hides the problem instead of fixing the design."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P198","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Claude returns several tool-use requests in one response, and they are independent of each other. Which TWO practices are correct for the application? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Execute only the first request and ignore the rest."},{"l":"B","t":"Execute the independent requests concurrently where it is safe to do so."},{"l":"C","t":"Send each result back in its own separate API call, one after another."},{"l":"D","t":"Return all the results together in the next message, each matched to its request."},{"l":"E","t":"Ask the user to choose which request should run."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"When Claude requests several independent tools, run them concurrently where safe and return all results together, each matched to its request.","x":["Incorrect. Ignoring the other independent requests leaves part of Claude's request unanswered, so the task cannot be completed correctly.","Correct. Independent requests can run concurrently where it is safe, which saves time.","Incorrect. Sending each result in its own separate call is not how results are returned. They go back together in the next message.","Correct. Return all the results together in the next message, each matched to its request.","Incorrect. The user should not have to choose which request runs. Claude asked for all of them."],"df":"hard","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P202","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A deployment shows normal latency and error rates, but a spot check finds that many answers are fluent and wrong. What monitoring was missing?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"More frequent CPU and memory monitoring on the application servers."},{"l":"B","t":"Ongoing quality monitoring: sampled production outputs graded by a calibrated judge or by people, tracked over time."},{"l":"C","t":"A higher error-rate threshold, so that alerts are not sent for small changes."},{"l":"D","t":"Monitoring of network latency between the application and the API, since slow connections are the usual cause of wrong answers."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Operational metrics cannot see wrong-but-fluent answers. Sampled quality grading of production outputs, tracked over time, catches silent quality failure.","x":["Incorrect. CPU and memory monitoring tells you about server health, not whether answers are right. Latency and errors were already normal.","Correct. Fluent but wrong answers are only visible if sampled production outputs are graded, by a calibrated judge or people, and tracked over time.","Incorrect. Raising an error-rate threshold reduces alerts and does nothing to detect wrong answers.","Incorrect. Latency was already normal, and slow connections do not cause wrong answers."],"df":"hard","ob":"Analyze observability challenges and select monitoring strategies at scale"},
{"id":"P206","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A team moves from loading all reference documents up front to progressive discovery. What new risk must it manage?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The model will be unable to use any document once progressive discovery is enabled."},{"l":"B","t":"The model may fail to find or load material it needs, and each load adds a step, so descriptions and evals matter."},{"l":"C","t":"Progressive discovery always costs more tokens than loading everything up front, because each document is described and then loaded."},{"l":"D","t":"Progressive discovery prevents prompt caching from working on any part of the request."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Progressive discovery trades context size for the risk that needed material is not found, plus an extra step for each load. Good descriptions and evals that check discovery manage the risk.","x":["Incorrect. Progressive discovery does not stop the model using documents. It loads them when needed.","Correct. The model may not find or load what it needs, and every load adds a step. Good descriptions and evals that check discovery manage that risk.","Incorrect. Progressive discovery usually saves tokens, because only the documents that are needed are loaded.","Incorrect. A stable prefix can still be cached, so progressive discovery does not prevent caching."],"df":"hard","ob":"Evaluate progressive discovery vs. monolithic context strategy"},
{"id":"P207","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A document-analysis job takes several minutes per request. The current design holds an HTTP connection open from the user's browser until it finishes, and many requests time out. Which design fits better?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise every timeout in the chain, from the browser to the load balancer and the backend, to thirty minutes so that jobs can finish."},{"l":"B","t":"Split each document into single pages and process them in the browser."},{"l":"C","t":"Accept the job, return a job ID, process it asynchronously, and notify or let the client poll for the result."},{"l":"D","t":"Ask users to submit only short documents."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Long-running work should be asynchronous: accept, return a job ID, process, and notify or allow polling. Long synchronous timeouts are fragile.","x":["Incorrect. Raising every timeout to thirty minutes keeps fragile long-held connections and users staring at a blank screen.","Incorrect. Processing pages in the browser does not fix the duration and moves the work to the client.","Correct. Accept the job, return a job ID, process it asynchronously, and let the client poll or be notified.","Incorrect. Limiting users to short documents removes the capability instead of fixing the design."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate accuracy-latency trade-offs and justify configuration decisions"},
{"id":"P209","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"A research agent can browse the web and send emails on the user's behalf. Which THREE controls are appropriate? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Treat web page content as untrusted input that can contain injected instructions."},{"l":"B","t":"Trust content from sites that use HTTPS, since encryption shows that the source is safe."},{"l":"C","t":"Require user confirmation before any email is sent."},{"l":"D","t":"Give the agent the user's full account credentials so that it never has to ask."},{"l":"E","t":"Restrict which recipients or domains the email tool can send to."}],"a":["A","C","E"],"r":"Web content is untrusted, so side-effecting actions need confirmation and a scope limit. HTTPS secures transport, not content, and full credentials maximise the damage an injection can do.","x":["Correct. Web page content is untrusted input and can contain injected instructions, so it must not be treated as commands.","Incorrect. HTTPS protects the connection, not the content, so a page served over HTTPS can still contain an injection.","Correct. Requiring user confirmation before any email is sent puts a gate in front of a side-effecting action.","Incorrect. Full credentials maximise the damage an injected instruction can do, so the agent should have only the access it needs.","Correct. Restricting recipients or domains limits where a manipulated agent could send information."],"df":"hard","ob":"Analyze authentication and authorization requirements to identify security gaps"},
{"id":"P210","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A CRM tool returns full customer records, including national ID numbers and bank details, although the agent only needs name, plan, and open tickets. What is the best change?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep the full record and ask the model not to mention sensitive fields."},{"l":"B","t":"Encrypt the full record in the tool output so that the model cannot read it."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep the full record but delete conversation logs after a week."},{"l":"D","t":"Change the tool so that it returns only the fields the agent's task needs."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Data minimisation at the tool boundary keeps sensitive fields out of context and logs altogether. Prompt instructions and short retention do not.","x":["Incorrect. A prompt instruction does not remove the sensitive fields from context, logs or the API request.","Incorrect. Encrypted output the model cannot read would also be unusable for the task, and the fields the agent needs still have to be readable.","Incorrect. Deleting logs after a week leaves the sensitive fields in context and logs for that week, and does not minimise exposure.","Correct. Data minimisation at the tool boundary returns only the fields the task needs, so sensitive data never reaches context or logs."],"df":"medium","ob":"Analyze authentication and authorization requirements to identify security gaps"},
{"id":"P211","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"An agent must create orders in a legacy system that only accepts complex XML messages over SOAP. Which integration fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Let the model write the raw SOAP XML itself and send it directly to the legacy endpoint, since it has seen many XML examples in training."},{"l":"B","t":"Wrap the legacy call in a thin service exposed as a tool with a simple typed schema, and validate before sending."},{"l":"C","t":"Replace the legacy system before the agent project can begin."},{"l":"D","t":"Have the model write the XML and email it to an operator who submits it by hand."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Wrap complex legacy protocols in a thin service with a simple typed tool schema and validation. The model fills in a few fields and code builds the message reliably.","x":["Incorrect. Writing raw SOAP XML directly and sending it to the legacy endpoint risks malformed messages reaching a system of record, with no validation.","Correct. Wrap the legacy call in a thin service exposed as a tool with a simple typed schema and validate before sending. The model fills a few fields and code builds the message.","Incorrect. Replacing the legacy system first is a huge prerequisite that the project does not need.","Incorrect. Emailing XML to an operator to submit by hand removes the automation and adds a manual step."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluate connection protocols and select the appropriate integration mechanism (MCP, API/CLI, agent-to-agent)"},
{"id":"P55","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A startup plans to build its claims-triage workflow first and 'add tests once it looks right'. Which recommendation best reflects good practice?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Build the prototype first, because the team needs a working system to know what is worth measuring."},{"l":"B","t":"Define success criteria, a golden dataset, and graders before production code so that evals gate every change."},{"l":"C","t":"Rely on a stakeholder demo as the acceptance test, since stakeholders know what good looks like."},{"l":"D","t":"Launch and use user complaints as the dataset, because real feedback is the most representative."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Writing evals first forces success to be stated in measurable terms, exposes assumptions while they are cheap to change, and creates a gate for every model swap, prompt change, or retrieval change.","x":["Incorrect. Building first leaves success undefined. Measurable criteria written first expose assumptions while they are cheap to change.","Correct. Success criteria, a golden dataset and graders defined before production code let evals gate every prompt, model or retrieval change.","Incorrect. A demo is not a test. It shows hand-picked cases and does not give repeatable acceptance criteria.","Incorrect. Using complaints as the dataset means users find the failures first, and the sample is biased toward the cases that were reported."],"df":"easy","ob":"Define evaluation metrics (accuracy, latency, cost, safety, security)"},
{"id":"P57","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"Which THREE practices make an LLM-as-judge more reliable? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Grade with a different model than the one that produced the outputs."},{"l":"B","t":"Use a detailed rubric with a small fixed set of verdict labels."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask for a free-form 1-100 score with no reasoning."},{"l":"D","t":"Calibrate the judge against human-labelled examples."},{"l":"E","t":"Use the same model that generated the answers to keep grading consistent."}],"a":["A","B","D"],"r":"Rigorous judging uses detailed rubrics, constrained verdicts, calibration against human labels, and a different grader to avoid self-preference. Free-form scores without reasoning hide inconsistency in borderline cases.","x":["Correct. A judge from a different model avoids self-preference, because a model tends to favour its own outputs.","Correct. A detailed rubric with a small fixed set of verdict labels makes judgements consistent and comparable.","Incorrect. A free-form 1-100 score without reasoning hides inconsistency in borderline cases and is hard to calibrate.","Correct. Calibrating the judge against human-labelled examples shows where it disagrees with people and lets you correct it.","Incorrect. The same model that wrote the answers tends to favour its own style, so it is a weaker grader."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P58","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A summarisation feature scores 97% on its eval set, yet users report failures on long, messy, multilingual inputs. The dataset contains only short, clean English samples. What is the most appropriate fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise the pass threshold on the existing dataset to 99% so that weaker results are rejected."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the failing user cases from the discussion as outliers that do not represent normal use."},{"l":"C","t":"Rescore the same dataset with an LLM judge in place of the current grading function."},{"l":"D","t":"Rebuild the dataset to reflect the real input distribution, including edge cases and adversarial inputs."}],"a":["D"],"r":"If the dataset is not representative, scores are not meaningful. The golden dataset must include the edge cases and counterexamples the system will actually meet.","x":["Incorrect. A higher threshold on a dataset that does not represent real inputs still measures the wrong thing.","Incorrect. Failures on long, messy and multilingual inputs are exactly what the dataset is missing. Dismissing them as outliers hides the problem.","Incorrect. A different grader on the same short, clean English samples still cannot reveal failures on inputs it does not contain.","Correct. The dataset must reflect the real input distribution, including edge cases and adversarial inputs, or the scores are not meaningful."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P59","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A prompt change raises the average eval score from 82 to 85, but the 'adversarial' category falls from 78 to 61. What should the team conclude?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Drop the adversarial category from the eval, since it is small and drags the results down."},{"l":"B","t":"Not clearly an improvement; review per-category results against the shipping criteria before deciding."},{"l":"C","t":"Rerun the eval repeatedly until the adversarial score recovers to its earlier level."},{"l":"D","t":"Ship the change, because the mean score improved and the mean is the headline metric."}],"a":["B"],"r":"A change that raises the mean while quietly degrading edge cases or adversarial inputs does not make the system better. Interpret per-category results, not just the aggregate.","x":["Incorrect. Dropping the adversarial category hides the regression instead of dealing with it.","Correct. The mean improved but the adversarial category fell sharply, so review per-category results against the shipping criteria before deciding.","Incorrect. Rerunning until the score recovers is selecting the result you want and not evidence.","Incorrect. A higher mean does not justify shipping when a category has degraded, because the mean can hide that."],"df":"medium","ob":"Define evaluation metrics (accuracy, latency, cost, safety, security)"},
{"id":"P60","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A team evaluates a model swap and sees a 4-point drop on the golden dataset. It then announces that a 5-point drop is 'acceptable'. What is wrong with this process?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The threshold was set after seeing the result, so the acceptance criteria were written after the build."},{"l":"B","t":"A 5-point threshold is too lenient for any model swap, so any drop at all should block the release."},{"l":"C","t":"Golden datasets cannot be used to compare two models, so the test itself is invalid."},{"l":"D","t":"Model swaps need no eval at all if the vendor states that the newer model is better."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Set the threshold before running the eval. A threshold chosen afterwards rationalises the outcome rather than gating it.","x":["Correct. The threshold was set after seeing the result, so it rationalises the outcome and does not gate it. Acceptance criteria belong before the run.","Incorrect. The issue is when the threshold was set, not that any drop must always block a release.","Incorrect. Golden datasets can be used to compare models, so the test is not invalid.","Incorrect. A vendor's claim does not replace evaluating the swap on your own dataset."],"df":"medium","ob":"Conduct A/B testing and iterative improvements"},
{"id":"P62","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"After two days of an A/B test the treatment is ahead and the team wants to stop and ship. What does good practice require?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Stop as soon as the treatment's difference first looks statistically significant, to limit exposure to the old prompt."},{"l":"B","t":"Compute sample size beforehand from baseline, minimum detectable effect, and confidence, and run to it."},{"l":"C","t":"Keep running until the result matches the hypothesis the team wrote down at the start of the test."},{"l":"D","t":"Skip the remainder of the test, because an early lead in a short window reliably proves the effect."}],"a":["B"],"r":"A team that runs until it sees what it wants will find it whether or not it is real. Sample size comes from the minimum detectable effect, the baseline, and the required confidence, and is larger for high-variance LLM outputs.","x":["Incorrect. Stopping at the first sign of significance inflates false positives, because repeated peeking finds an apparent effect that is not real.","Correct. Compute the sample size beforehand from the baseline, minimum detectable effect and required confidence, and run to it. LLM outputs are high variance, so the sample is larger.","Incorrect. Running until the result matches the hypothesis will find that result whether or not it is real.","Incorrect. An early lead in a short window does not prove the effect, and can come from chance or a novelty effect."],"df":"medium","ob":"Conduct A/B testing and iterative improvements"},
{"id":"P65","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A team switched to a cheaper model tier without re-evaluating. Format-compliance failures rose from 1% to 9% that week, with no prompt changes. Which failure class and fix apply?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"**Rate limiting:** add exponential backoff so that failed responses are retried until they are valid."},{"l":"B","t":"**Prompt failure:** the prompt was always ambiguous, so the team should rewrite it from scratch."},{"l":"C","t":"**Hallucination:** add retrieval over the format specification so that the model can look up the required format."},{"l":"D","t":"**Model mismatch:** the swap skipped the eval gate; re-evaluate tiers against the golden dataset."}],"a":["D"],"r":"The tier is wrong for the task, or was swapped without re-evaluation, so the fix is in model selection gated by an eval. Nothing else changed, which points away from prompt and retrieval causes.","x":["Incorrect. Rate limiting returns errors, not malformed output, and retrying does not make a weaker model follow the format.","Incorrect. The prompt had not changed and the failures rose only after the model switch, so the cause is the swap rather than the prompt.","Incorrect. Retrieval over the format specification addresses missing knowledge, not format compliance by a weaker tier.","Correct. This is a model mismatch. The tier was swapped without an eval gate, so re-evaluate the tiers against the golden dataset."],"df":"medium","ob":"Diagnose system issues (prompt failure, hallucinations, model mismatch)"},
{"id":"P66","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A RAG assistant's answer quality has slowly declined as the document corpus doubled over six months. Latency, model version, and prompts are unchanged. What should be investigated first, and how?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The temperature setting, by lowering it so that answers vary less as the corpus grows."},{"l":"B","t":"Network bandwidth between regions, by adding a content-delivery network in front of the API."},{"l":"C","t":"The model's reasoning ability, by switching to a larger tier and comparing its answers on a sample."},{"l":"D","t":"Retrieval quality: measure precision and recall on a labelled query set and check indexing of new documents."}],"a":["D"],"r":"RAG's characteristic failure is retrieval quality drift as documents are added without consistent indexing. Treat retrieval precision and recall as system metrics, not just output quality.","x":["Incorrect. Temperature has nothing to do with a corpus that has grown, so lowering it would not fix the decline.","Incorrect. Network bandwidth affects speed, not quality, and latency is unchanged.","Incorrect. The model and prompts are unchanged, so switching tier does not address a growing corpus.","Correct. Retrieval quality drifts as documents are added. Measure precision and recall on a labelled query set and check how new documents are indexed."],"df":"medium","ob":"Diagnose system issues (prompt failure, hallucinations, model mismatch)"},
{"id":"P68","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A team's demo shows a 1.2-second median response time. The product SLA is user-facing and the workload will be concurrent. Which latency measure should anchor the target?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The average of the fastest half of requests, because it removes outliers that distort the picture."},{"l":"B","t":"Median latency measured in single-request demos, because it shows the typical user experience."},{"l":"C","t":"p95 under realistic concurrent load, because the slow tail drives SLA breaches and abandonment."},{"l":"D","t":"The minimum observed latency, because it shows what the system can achieve when everything works."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Latency p95 under concurrent load is a different number from median latency under a single request, and the tail is what users and SLAs feel.","x":["Incorrect. Averaging the fastest half removes the slow tail, which is what users and SLAs feel.","Incorrect. A median from single-request demos does not reflect concurrent load or the slow tail.","Correct. p95 under realistic concurrent load captures the slow tail that causes SLA breaches and abandonment.","Incorrect. The minimum shows the best case only and says nothing about what most users experience."],"df":"easy","ob":"Optimize token usage, latency, and cost-performance trade-offs"},
{"id":"P70","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"An insurer's system extracts claim fields, summarises the narrative for an adjuster, and must never auto-deny a claim. Which eval design matches methods to behaviours best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"An LLM judge for every behaviour, since it is the most flexible method and avoids maintaining code."},{"l":"B","t":"Human review of every item produced, because the system handles claims and mistakes are costly."},{"l":"C","t":"Code-based exact-match scoring on the narrative summaries, comparing each against a reference text."},{"l":"D","t":"Code checks for no denial action and field accuracy; an LLM judge for summary faithfulness."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Use the cheapest reliable method per behaviour: binary and structured checks in code, interpretive judgments such as faithfulness with a calibrated judge. Exact-match on free-text summaries is not meaningful.","x":["Incorrect. An LLM judge for every behaviour is costly and unnecessary. Binary and structured checks are more reliable in code.","Incorrect. Human review of every item is the most expensive method and does not scale. Use it selectively.","Incorrect. Exact match against a reference text is not meaningful for free-text summaries, which can be correct in many wordings.","Correct. Use code checks for the no-denial action and field accuracy, and a calibrated LLM judge for summary faithfulness. Each behaviour gets the cheapest reliable method."],"df":"medium","ob":"Define evaluation metrics (accuracy, latency, cost, safety, security)"},
{"id":"P214","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants a model judge to compare two prompt versions on open-ended answers. Which judging design is most reliable?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask the judge to give each answer an absolute score from 1 to 100 with no explanation."},{"l":"B","t":"Always show version A first and version B second, so that the order is consistent."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the judge which answer is longer, since length is a fair proxy for completeness."},{"l":"D","t":"Pairwise comparison against a rubric, with the order of the two answers randomised or swapped."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Pairwise comparison against a rubric is more reliable than absolute scores, and swapping or randomising order controls position bias. Length is not quality.","x":["Incorrect. An absolute 1-100 score with no explanation is unstable and hard to calibrate for open-ended answers.","Incorrect. Always showing version A first bakes position bias into every comparison.","Incorrect. Length is not a measure of quality or completeness.","Correct. Pairwise comparison against a rubric, with the order randomised or swapped, is more reliable and controls position bias."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P218","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"An eval scores unusually high. A reviewer notices that several eval cases are word-for-word copies of the few-shot examples in the prompt. What is the problem?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Data leakage: the eval includes cases the prompt already shows, so the score overstates real performance."},{"l":"B","t":"The few-shot examples are too short, so the model is copying them."},{"l":"C","t":"The eval set is too large, so some overlap is unavoidable and harmless."},{"l":"D","t":"The judge model is too lenient on these cases and should be replaced with a stricter judge from a different model family."}],"a":["A"],"r":"If eval cases appear in the prompt, the model is being tested on answers it was shown. Keep eval data separate from prompt examples.","x":["Correct. This is data leakage. The eval contains cases the prompt already shows, so the score overstates real performance.","Incorrect. Short examples are not the issue. The problem is that eval cases were also in the prompt.","Incorrect. Overlap with the prompt's examples is not harmless, and set size does not make it unavoidable.","Incorrect. A stricter judge does not remove leaked cases, so the score still overstates performance."],"df":"medium","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P224","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"An agent often calls `search_orders` when it should call `get_order`, even when the user gives an exact order ID. The two tool descriptions are almost identical. What is the most likely cause and fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Hallucination; add retrieval so that the model knows which orders exist."},{"l":"B","t":"Model mismatch; move the agent to a larger model tier so that it chooses between similar tools more carefully on every request."},{"l":"C","t":"Overlapping tool descriptions; make each description state clearly when to use it and when to use the other."},{"l":"D","t":"Rate limiting; add backoff so that the correct tool has time to respond."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Near-identical descriptions leave the model guessing. Each description should say when to use the tool and how it differs from similar ones.","x":["Incorrect. The agent is not inventing facts. It is picking between two similar tools, so retrieval of order data does not address the cause.","Incorrect. A larger tier may help a little, but the cause is almost identical descriptions, which a bigger model also has to guess between.","Correct. Overlapping descriptions leave the model guessing. Each should state when to use it and how it differs from the similar tool.","Incorrect. Rate limiting returns errors, and does not cause the model to pick the wrong tool."],"df":"medium","ob":"Diagnose system issues (prompt failure, hallucinations, model mismatch)"},
{"id":"P226","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"An assistant performs well in English but poorly in Hindi. The golden dataset contains only English cases, and the release passed every threshold. What is the root problem?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The model cannot handle Hindi, so Hindi support should be withdrawn."},{"l":"B","t":"The eval did not cover a population the system serves; add a Hindi slice with its own threshold."},{"l":"C","t":"The thresholds were too low; raising them on the English set will fix Hindi quality."},{"l":"D","t":"The judge model was biased against Hindi, so it should be replaced."}],"a":["B"],"r":"An eval only tells you about the populations it contains. Add slices for every population served, each with its own threshold.","x":["Incorrect. Poor Hindi performance is not proof that the model cannot handle Hindi. The gap is that nothing in the eval measured it.","Correct. The golden dataset did not cover a population the system serves. Add a Hindi slice with its own threshold.","Incorrect. Raising thresholds on an English-only set cannot show or improve Hindi quality.","Incorrect. Nothing shows the judge is biased. With no Hindi cases, the eval had no way to detect the problem."],"df":"hard","ob":"Design evaluation datasets and test frameworks using mixed methodologies"},
{"id":"P228","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Output style changed overnight with no code or prompt change. The application refers to the model by an alias that now points to a newer version. What is the best practice?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep using the alias, so that the application always gets the latest model version automatically without any extra work."},{"l":"B","t":"Lower the temperature so that the new version behaves like the old one."},{"l":"C","t":"Pin a specific model version in production and move to a new one only after it passes the eval."},{"l":"D","t":"Rewrite the prompt from scratch whenever the style changes."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Aliases can move to new versions. Production should pin a version and treat each move as a release gated by the eval.","x":["Incorrect. An alias that moves to a newer version changes behaviour without any release process, which caused this change.","Incorrect. Temperature does not make a new model version behave like the old one.","Correct. Pin a specific model version in production and move to a new one only after it passes the eval.","Incorrect. Rewriting the prompt each time the style changes treats the symptom and does not control the cause."],"df":"medium","ob":"Diagnose system issues (prompt failure, hallucinations, model mismatch)"},
{"id":"P229","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO changes can reduce latency without risking answer quality? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Cache a long, stable prompt prefix so that it is not reprocessed on every request."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the guardrail checks that run before the answer is shown."},{"l":"C","t":"Cut `max_tokens` below the length that a complete answer needs."},{"l":"D","t":"Run independent retrieval and tool calls in parallel instead of in sequence."},{"l":"E","t":"Skip the eval for configuration changes that affect latency only."}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Caching a stable prefix and parallelising independent calls cut latency without changing what the model sees. Removing checks, truncating output, or skipping evals trade quality for speed.","x":["Correct. Caching a long stable prefix avoids reprocessing it each request, which cuts latency without changing the content.","Incorrect. Removing guardrail checks trades safety for speed.","Incorrect. Cutting max_tokens below the length of a complete answer truncates answers and hurts quality.","Correct. Running independent retrieval and tool calls in parallel means the slowest call sets the wait and not their sum.","Incorrect. Skipping the eval for latency-only changes can let a quality regression through."],"df":"hard","ob":"Optimize token usage, latency, and cost-performance trade-offs"},
{"id":"P230","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A report-writing feature produces long, padded outputs. Cost analysis shows output tokens dominate the bill. Which change targets this directly?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Shorten the system prompt instead, since input tokens are what drive the cost of a long report in most applications."},{"l":"B","t":"Specify a concise output format and target length, and check with the eval that quality holds."},{"l":"C","t":"Move to a larger tier so that outputs are more efficient."},{"l":"D","t":"Remove prompt caching, because it makes outputs longer."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Output tokens are priced higher than input tokens. A concise format and target length reduce them directly, with the eval confirming quality.","x":["Incorrect. Shortening the system prompt addresses input tokens, but the cost analysis shows output tokens dominate.","Correct. A concise output format and a target length cut output tokens directly, and the eval checks that quality holds.","Incorrect. A larger tier costs more per token and does not make outputs shorter.","Incorrect. Prompt caching affects input cost and does not make outputs longer."],"df":"medium","ob":"Optimize token usage, latency, and cost-performance trade-offs"},
{"id":"P236","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team wants to keep prompts and responses for debugging, but they may contain personal data. Which TWO practices fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Store everything in plaintext with open access, so that debugging is fast."},{"l":"B","t":"Disable all logging, so that no personal data can ever be stored."},{"l":"C","t":"Redact or tokenise sensitive fields before logs are written."},{"l":"D","t":"Restrict access to the logs and apply a defined retention period."},{"l":"E","t":"Copy logs to personal laptops so that engineers can work offline."}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Redact before writing, then limit access and retention. Disabling logging blinds debugging, and open or copied logs spread personal data.","x":["Incorrect. Plaintext with open access exposes personal data to anyone, which is a privacy failure.","Incorrect. Disabling all logging removes the ability to debug, which the team needs.","Correct. Redacting or tokenising sensitive fields before the logs are written keeps debugging useful while reducing exposure.","Correct. Restricted access and a defined retention period limit who can see the logs and for how long.","Incorrect. Copying logs to personal laptops spreads personal data outside controlled systems."],"df":"medium","ob":"Monitor system performance using logging and observability tools"},
{"id":"P237","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"Offline evals passed, but the team wants ongoing evidence of quality in production. Which approach fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sample a share of production traffic, grade it with a calibrated judge, and track the trend against thresholds."},{"l":"B","t":"Re-run the offline eval once a quarter and assume production matches it."},{"l":"C","t":"Grade every production request with a trained person, regardless of volume, so that no wrong answer can go unnoticed."},{"l":"D","t":"Rely on the error rate, since wrong answers usually cause errors."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Sampled online grading with a calibrated judge, tracked against thresholds, gives continuous quality evidence at reasonable cost.","x":["Correct. Sample a share of production traffic, grade it with a calibrated judge and track the trend against thresholds. This gives continuous evidence at reasonable cost.","Incorrect. A quarterly offline re-run assumes production matches it and misses drift between runs.","Incorrect. Grading every production request by person does not scale and is very costly.","Incorrect. Wrong answers usually do not cause errors, so the error rate can look fine while quality drops."],"df":"hard","ob":"Monitor system performance using logging and observability tools"},
{"id":"P240","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"A release review must decide whether a new configuration ships. Which THREE items belong in the eval report? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Per-category scores compared with the thresholds agreed in advance."},{"l":"B","t":"Only the aggregate score, so that the decision is simple."},{"l":"C","t":"Comparison with the current baseline on the same dataset version."},{"l":"D","t":"Screenshots of a few good answers chosen by the team."},{"l":"E","t":"The cost and latency differences against the baseline."}],"a":["A","C","E"],"r":"A release decision needs per-category results against pre-agreed thresholds, a like-for-like baseline comparison, and cost and latency effects. Aggregates and chosen screenshots hide regressions.","x":["Correct. Per-category scores against thresholds agreed in advance show where the change helps or hurts.","Incorrect. An aggregate-only report hides regressions in individual categories.","Correct. A comparison with the current baseline on the same dataset version shows whether the change is an improvement.","Incorrect. Screenshots of a few good answers chosen by the team are selective and not evidence.","Correct. Cost and latency differences against the baseline are part of the release decision."],"df":"hard","ob":"Define evaluation metrics (accuracy, latency, cost, safety, security)"},
{"id":"P73","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A wealth-management assistant must never recommend products on the firm's restricted list. Claude's trained behaviour already declines broadly harmful content. Where must this rule be enforced?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"In Claude's trained behaviour, since alignment training covers each firm's own product restrictions."},{"l":"B","t":"Nowhere in the system, because a list this specific cannot be enforced reliably by software."},{"l":"C","t":"In the application layer, with deterministic checks, because training never saw the firm's list."},{"l":"D","t":"In a single reminder line in the system prompt, because instructions are enough to enforce firm policy."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Training reduces broad harm classes; your domain policy, data rules, and authorization model are yours to enforce. The dangerous failure is silent: assuming Claude enforces a rule it was never given.","x":["Incorrect. Alignment training reduces broad harm classes. It never saw this firm's restricted list.","Incorrect. A specific list can be enforced reliably with deterministic checks, so it is not unenforceable.","Correct. The application layer enforces domain policy with deterministic checks, because training never saw the firm's list.","Incorrect. A single instruction line is a request, not enforcement. The model can still ignore it."],"df":"easy","ob":"Implement guardrails and safety controls"},
{"id":"P78","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Attackers submit enormous, padded documents that inflate cost and cause responses to truncate. Which control directly addresses this risk category?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"An output toxicity classifier that checks every response before it reaches the end user."},{"l":"B","t":"Input size limits, chunking, and budget monitoring with alerts on tokens per request."},{"l":"C","t":"Single sign-on for end users, so that every request can be tied to a known identity."},{"l":"D","t":"A deterministic allowlist for the tools the agent is permitted to call on behalf of users."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Token-budget exhaustion is its own risk category: oversized or padded inputs consume the context or output budget. Limits and monitoring address it; content classifiers and tool allowlists address other risks.","x":["Incorrect. A toxicity classifier addresses harmful content, not oversized padded input that inflates cost.","Correct. Input size limits, chunking and budget monitoring with alerts on tokens per request directly address token-budget exhaustion.","Incorrect. Single sign-on ties requests to an identity but does not limit how big a request can be.","Incorrect. A tool allowlist limits which tools can be called, which is a different risk from oversized inputs."],"df":"medium","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P79","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO pairings of decision type and review placement are appropriate? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Every decision of every kind, with pre-action approval."},{"l":"B","t":"An irreversible account closure flagged at low confidence, with pre-action approval."},{"l":"C","t":"A benefit denial near the eligibility threshold, with no review because confidence is 0.92."},{"l":"D","t":"Reversible subject-line suggestions for marketing emails, with post-action audit."},{"l":"E","t":"A medication-dose recommendation, with sampled review only."}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Pre-action approval suits irreversible, high-cost cases; post-action audit suits reversible, lower-cost ones. Sampled review monitors the system rather than guarding individual outcomes, high confidence does not lower the stakes of an irreversible harm, and reviewing everything overwhelms reviewers.","x":["Incorrect. Reviewing every decision before action overwhelms reviewers and slows the low-risk cases that do not need it.","Correct. An irreversible account closure flagged at low confidence needs pre-action approval, because it cannot be undone afterwards.","Incorrect. A denial near the eligibility threshold is a high-stakes decision, and high confidence does not lower the stakes. It should not go without review.","Correct. Reversible subject-line suggestions are low cost, so a post-action audit is enough.","Incorrect. Sampled review monitors the system but does not guard an individual dose recommendation, where a wrong answer can cause harm."],"df":"hard","ob":"Apply human-in-the-loop validation strategies"},
{"id":"P80","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"To be safe, a team routes every AI decision to human approval. Within weeks reviewers are approving nearly everything within seconds. What failure is this, and what is the remedy?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"**Prompt injection:** attackers are steering the reviewers, so an input classifier should be added."},{"l":"B","t":"**Consent fatigue:** route by confidence, reversibility, and cost of error, not by volume."},{"l":"C","t":"**Token exhaustion:** the reviewer interface is too verbose, so its content should be shortened."},{"l":"D","t":"**Model drift:** the model's behaviour has changed, so it should be retrained on the reviewers' decisions."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Routing everything to humans floods the queue until reviewers click through without reading. Focus review effort on the decisions that warrant it.","x":["Incorrect. The pattern is reviewers approving everything quickly, which points to fatigue and not to an attack on the reviewers.","Correct. This is consent fatigue. Route by confidence, reversibility and cost of error rather than sending everything to a person.","Incorrect. Reviewers approving quickly is not about interface verbosity, and token exhaustion is a different risk.","Incorrect. The behaviour at issue is the reviewers', not the model's, so retraining on their decisions would copy the click-through habit."],"df":"easy","ob":"Apply human-in-the-loop validation strategies"},
{"id":"P81","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A hospital holds a signed BAA for its first-party API configuration. A developer wants to use a beta feature with PHI. What should the architect do?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Confirm that the feature is explicitly listed as BAA-eligible, since beta features are generally excluded."},{"l":"B","t":"Move the team to a consumer Claude.ai plan, which has simpler terms for handling health data."},{"l":"C","t":"Proceed with the beta feature, because a signed BAA covers every feature offered on that configuration."},{"l":"D","t":"Proceed as long as the processing region is pinned to the United States, since residency satisfies HIPAA."}],"a":["A"],"r":"BAA coverage is configuration- and feature-specific. Eligible features must be confirmed; beta features are generally excluded unless explicitly listed.","x":["Correct. BAA coverage is specific to configuration and feature. Confirm the feature is explicitly listed as eligible, because beta features are generally excluded.","Incorrect. A consumer plan does not suit PHI, and a BAA for the API configuration does not carry over to it.","Incorrect. A signed BAA does not automatically cover every feature, including beta ones.","Incorrect. Pinning the region does not by itself satisfy HIPAA, and residency does not make a beta feature eligible."],"df":"medium","ob":"Ensure compliance with regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, FedRAMP)"},
{"id":"P84","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"Where can unequal outcomes enter a Claude-based decision system? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Downstream routing that sends some groups down different paths."},{"l":"B","t":"Few-shot examples that carry the same skew."},{"l":"C","t":"The TLS cipher suite used to encrypt the API traffic between the application and the provider."},{"l":"D","t":"A retrieval corpus that over- or under-represents some groups."},{"l":"E","t":"The number of retries configured on the client for transient network errors."}],"a":["A","B","D"],"r":"The four identifiable entry points are the retrieval corpus, prompt framing, few-shot examples, and downstream routing. Naming them makes fairness something you can instrument rather than assume the provider handles.","x":["Correct. Downstream routing that sends some groups down different paths can produce unequal outcomes.","Correct. Few-shot examples that carry a skew teach the same skew to the model.","Incorrect. A cipher suite protects traffic in transit and has no effect on fairness of decisions.","Correct. A retrieval corpus that over- or under-represents some groups can bias what the model sees.","Incorrect. The number of retries for network errors does not influence whether outcomes differ across groups."],"df":"medium","ob":"Address ethical AI considerations (bias, fairness, transparency)"},
{"id":"P87","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A lending assistant's decisions have to be explainable. Which description of the explanations the system must support is correct?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"One record serves several audiences: the applicant's reason, the regulator's reconstruction, the team's debugging."},{"l":"B","t":"One explanation written for the regulator is sufficient, since the regulator is the most demanding of the audiences."},{"l":"C","t":"Explanations can be produced on request by asking the model why it decided as it did once the decision is made."},{"l":"D","t":"Explainability is a property of the model the vendor supplies, so it is settled during model selection."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Fairness and transparency are instrumented rather than assumed. Decision logging is built once and serves the affected person, the regulator, the build team, and the control register, each taking a different explanation from the same record. Asking the model to rationalise afterwards is not a reconstruction of what happened.","x":["Correct. One decision record serves several audiences, including the applicant's reason, the regulator's reconstruction and the team's debugging.","Incorrect. A single explanation for the regulator does not give the applicant a reason or help the team debug.","Incorrect. Asking the model afterwards produces a plausible story and not a reconstruction of what happened, so it cannot be relied on.","Incorrect. Explainability is built into the system through decision logging and design, not settled by choosing a model."],"df":"hard","ob":"Address ethical AI considerations (bias, fairness, transparency)"},
{"id":"P246","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team plans to put an internal API key and database password in the system prompt so that the agent can use them. What is the problem?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"There is no problem, since users cannot see the system prompt and the model will only use the secrets through its tools."},{"l":"B","t":"The system prompt will become too long, which increases latency."},{"l":"C","t":"System prompts can be extracted; keep secrets in the application and have tools use them server-side."},{"l":"D","t":"Secrets in the system prompt prevent prompt caching from working."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Assume the system prompt can be extracted. Secrets stay in the application, and tools use them server-side without the model seeing them.","x":["Incorrect. System prompts can be extracted, so users may be able to obtain the secrets.","Incorrect. Length is not the main problem. Exposure of secrets is.","Correct. Assume the system prompt can be extracted. Keep secrets in the application and have the tools use them server-side.","Incorrect. A stable prompt can still be cached. The issue is secret exposure."],"df":"medium","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P250","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Any employee can edit the knowledge base that the support agent retrieves from. Someone adds a false refund policy, and the agent repeats it to customers. Which control addresses the cause?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Content governance for the knowledge base: approval for edits, provenance tracking, and change history."},{"l":"B","t":"A larger model, on the basis that a more capable agent will notice which of the retrieved refund policies is the false one."},{"l":"C","t":"An output filter that blocks the word 'refund'."},{"l":"D","t":"A disclaimer telling customers that policies may be inaccurate."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Retrieved content is only as trustworthy as its governance. Approval, provenance, and history stop false content entering the corpus.","x":["Correct. The cause is uncontrolled editing. Approval for edits, provenance tracking and change history stop false content entering the corpus.","Incorrect. A larger model has no way of knowing which retrieved policy is false. It will repeat what the knowledge base says.","Incorrect. Blocking the word 'refund' stops legitimate answers too and does not fix the source of the false policy.","Incorrect. A disclaimer does not correct the false policy, and customers are still misled."],"df":"medium","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P251","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"An agent built to summarise tickets was given tools to close tickets, issue credits, and email customers, 'in case they are useful'. Which risk does this create, and what is the fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Hallucination; ground the summaries with retrieval."},{"l":"B","t":"Token exhaustion; limit the length of each summary so that the agent has fewer tokens in which to take unexpected actions."},{"l":"C","t":"Excessive agency; remove tools the task does not need and gate any remaining consequential actions."},{"l":"D","t":"Data residency; pin the agent to a single region."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Tools beyond the task's needs create excessive agency. Remove them and gate the consequential actions that remain.","x":["Incorrect. The summaries are not the issue. The risk is the powerful tools the agent does not need.","Incorrect. Shorter summaries do not reduce what the agent is allowed to do with its tools.","Correct. Tools beyond the task's needs create excessive agency. Remove what is not needed and gate any consequential action that remains.","Incorrect. Data residency concerns where data is processed, not what actions the agent can take."],"df":"hard","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P252","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A compliance assistant answers questions about regulations, some of which changed after the model's training data was collected. What is the right design response?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell users that the model knows all current regulations."},{"l":"B","t":"Ground answers in current, retrieved regulatory text and show its effective date."},{"l":"C","t":"Wait for a newer model and make no design changes in the meantime."},{"l":"D","t":"Raise the temperature so that the model is less attached to its older knowledge."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Training data has a cutoff. Ground answers in current retrieved text and show effective dates so that users can judge currency.","x":["Incorrect. Telling users the model knows all current regulations is false, since its training data has a cutoff.","Correct. Ground answers in current retrieved regulatory text and show its effective date, so users can judge how current it is.","Incorrect. Waiting for a newer model leaves the same cutoff problem in place, and users get outdated answers meanwhile.","Incorrect. Temperature changes randomness and does not supply newer regulations."],"df":"medium","ob":"Identify risks, limitations, and failure modes of LLM systems"},
{"id":"P255","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team sets a confidence threshold for sending extractions to human review. How should the threshold be chosen?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Pick 0.9, because it is a round number that sounds safe."},{"l":"B","t":"Use the model's own confidence scores exactly as they are given, without checking them, since the model knows best how sure it is."},{"l":"C","t":"Use calibration data relating confidence to actual error rates, and set the threshold for acceptable risk and review capacity."},{"l":"D","t":"Set the threshold so that exactly half of the items go to review."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Thresholds should come from calibration data linking confidence to actual errors, balanced against acceptable risk and reviewer capacity.","x":["Incorrect. A round number is arbitrary and not tied to how errors actually behave.","Incorrect. Model confidence scores are not reliably calibrated, so they need to be checked against actual outcomes.","Correct. Calibration data linking confidence to actual error rates, balanced against acceptable risk and review capacity, gives a defensible threshold.","Incorrect. A fixed fifty per cent ignores the real error distribution and may overwhelm reviewers or miss errors."],"df":"medium","ob":"Apply human-in-the-loop validation strategies"},
{"id":"P257","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A customer exercises their right to have their personal data erased. Their data appears in application logs, the retrieval index, and cached conversation summaries. What is required?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Delete the customer's record in the CRM only, since that is the system of record from which all the other stores are derived."},{"l":"B","t":"A deletion process that finds and removes the data across all stores, including logs, indexes, and caches."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the model to forget the customer."},{"l":"D","t":"Nothing, since data used by AI systems is exempt from erasure requests."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Erasure must reach every copy of the data, including logs, indexes, and caches. Map these stores at design time so that requests can be met.","x":["Incorrect. Deleting only the CRM record leaves copies in logs, indexes and caches.","Correct. Erasure must reach every copy, including logs, indexes and caches, so a deletion process across all stores is required.","Incorrect. A model cannot be asked to forget someone, since the data is in the stores around it.","Incorrect. Data used by AI systems is not exempt from erasure requests."],"df":"medium","ob":"Ensure compliance with regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, FedRAMP)"},
{"id":"P259","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A clinical assistant drafts appointment reminders. The team plans to include each patient's full medical history in every prompt 'for context'. Which principle applies?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Full history should always be included, since more context improves quality."},{"l":"B","t":"Data residency, which is the only HIPAA requirement relevant to prompts."},{"l":"C","t":"Minimum necessary: use only the PHI the task requires."},{"l":"D","t":"None, as long as a BAA is in place."}],"a":["C"],"r":"HIPAA's minimum necessary standard limits PHI to what the task requires. A BAA does not remove that obligation.","x":["Incorrect. More context is not always better, and sending full medical history for a reminder exposes patient data without need.","Incorrect. Data residency is not the only HIPAA requirement relevant to prompts, and it does not address how much PHI is sent.","Correct. The minimum necessary principle limits PHI to what the task requires, so an appointment reminder needs far less than a full history.","Incorrect. A BAA does not remove the minimum necessary obligation."],"df":"medium","ob":"Ensure compliance with regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, FedRAMP)"},
{"id":"P261","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team assumes that the provider retains no request data, because it saw this on a forum. Which action is correct before the design is approved?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Confirm the provider's retention terms for the specific route, contract, and features in use, and record the evidence."},{"l":"B","t":"Proceed on the assumption, since forum posts usually reflect current policy."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask Claude what the provider's data-retention policy is, and record its answer in the design document as the supporting evidence."},{"l":"D","t":"Assume all providers retain data forever and drop the project."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Retention terms depend on route, contract, and feature. Confirm them from authoritative sources and keep the evidence. A model's answer is not evidence.","x":["Correct. Retention depends on route, contract and feature, so confirm the terms for those in use and record the evidence.","Incorrect. Forum posts are not authoritative and may be outdated or wrong.","Incorrect. A model's answer about the provider's policy is not evidence and may be wrong.","Incorrect. Assuming all providers retain data forever is another guess, and dropping the project without checking is unjustified."],"df":"medium","ob":"Ensure compliance with regulations (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA, FedRAMP)"},
{"id":"P264","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants to test whether its CV-screening assistant treats candidates differently by gender. Which test fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask the model whether it is biased and record its answer."},{"l":"B","t":"Check that the system prompt contains a line forbidding bias."},{"l":"C","t":"Counterfactual tests: submit matched CVs that differ only in gender-linked details and compare the outcomes."},{"l":"D","t":"Review a handful of decisions by hand each month and look for anything that seems unfair to the reviewer at the time."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Counterfactual tests isolate the attribute by changing only it. Self-reports and prompt lines are not measurements.","x":["Incorrect. A model's own statement that it is not biased is not a measurement.","Incorrect. A prompt line forbidding bias does not test whether outcomes differ.","Correct. Counterfactual tests change only the gender-linked details of otherwise matched CVs and compare outcomes, which isolates the effect.","Incorrect. A handful of manual reviews depends on what the reviewer happens to notice, and does not isolate the attribute."],"df":"medium","ob":"Address ethical AI considerations (bias, fairness, transparency)"},
{"id":"P267","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"Teams across a company want to use an internal Claude assistant for new purposes. What documentation helps them use it safely?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A list of the prompts used, with no further explanation."},{"l":"B","t":"A system card describing intended uses, known limitations, eval results, and uses that are not allowed."},{"l":"C","t":"The provider's marketing materials about Claude's capabilities."},{"l":"D","t":"No documentation is needed, since each team can test the assistant themselves and learn its limits by using it for a while."}],"a":["B"],"r":"A system card covering intended uses, limitations, eval results, and prohibited uses helps new teams adopt the assistant safely.","x":["Incorrect. A list of prompts without explanation does not tell teams what the assistant is for or where it fails.","Correct. A system card covering intended uses, known limitations, eval results and prohibited uses lets new teams adopt the assistant safely.","Incorrect. Marketing materials about capabilities do not describe this deployment's limits or rules.","Incorrect. Teams learning limits by trial exposes customers to the failures and is not documentation."],"df":"medium","ob":"Address ethical AI considerations (bias, fairness, transparency)"},
{"id":"P268","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"An assistant is found to have given dangerous advice to several customers. Which THREE steps belong in the response? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Contain the problem, for example by disabling the affected feature with a flag."},{"l":"B","t":"Delete the logs of the affected conversations to limit exposure."},{"l":"C","t":"Preserve the evidence and find the root cause."},{"l":"D","t":"Quietly change the prompt and tell no one."},{"l":"E","t":"Add the failing cases to the eval so that the fix is verified and the problem cannot return."}],"a":["A","C","E"],"r":"Contain, preserve evidence and find the root cause, then lock in the fix with eval cases. Deleting logs or silent changes destroy accountability.","x":["Correct. Containment, for example disabling the affected feature with a flag, stops further harm.","Incorrect. Deleting logs destroys evidence and accountability, and does not reduce the harm.","Correct. Preserve the evidence and find the root cause so the fix addresses the real problem.","Incorrect. Quietly changing the prompt without telling anyone removes accountability and hides the incident.","Correct. Adding the failing cases to the eval verifies the fix and keeps the problem from coming back."],"df":"hard","ob":"Implement guardrails and safety controls"},
{"id":"P88","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A sponsor says, 'We want this to feel seamless.' Which translation is best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Promise zero downtime in the statement of work so that the sponsor's expectation is met in writing."},{"l":"B","t":"Set it aside as a subjective preference that cannot be turned into a requirement or a design constraint."},{"l":"C","t":"Select the largest available model tier, since 'seamless' means the highest possible quality of response."},{"l":"D","t":"Treat it as a latency and graceful-failure requirement: set a p95 target and confirm the assumption."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Discovery listens, translates, and writes down. 'Seamless' implies a latency budget and failures that do not break the flow, and the assumption is recorded and confirmed.","x":["Incorrect. A zero-downtime promise written into the statement of work commits the team to something no system can guarantee. The sponsor's wish should be translated into a measurable target.","Incorrect. A subjective phrase can be turned into requirements. 'Seamless' points to latency and failure behaviour that can be specified.","Incorrect. Quality is not what 'seamless' means, and the largest tier adds latency and cost, which works against a seamless feel.","Correct. 'Seamless' implies a latency budget and failures that do not break the flow. Set a p95 target, record the assumption and confirm it with the sponsor."],"df":"easy","ob":"Conduct structured discovery and requirement gathering"},
{"id":"P90","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A sponsor says, 'Clinicians will review the output anyway.' How should the architect translate this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Reduce evaluation effort, because clinicians will catch any errors before they reach the patient record."},{"l":"B","t":"Remove detailed logging, because a named person is accountable for each note that is authorised."},{"l":"C","t":"Assume the review is optional and can be skipped when clinicians are under time pressure."},{"l":"D","t":"A mandatory human authorization gate before output enters the record; confirm authority and timing."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Where output has legal, financial, or clinical consequence, review is an architectural gate, not an informal safety net. Record the assumption and confirm authority and timing.","x":["Incorrect. Expecting clinicians to catch errors is not a reason to evaluate less. The review step has to be designed and tested.","Incorrect. A named person being accountable is a reason to keep detailed logs, since the record must show who authorised what.","Incorrect. Treating review as optional leaves nothing between a wrong output and the patient record when clinicians are busy.","Correct. Where output has clinical consequence, review is an architectural gate. Make authorisation mandatory before output enters the record and confirm who has the authority and when."],"df":"medium","ob":"Conduct structured discovery and requirement gathering"},
{"id":"P93","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"Which quality SLA threshold is most defensible?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The best score observed during the pilot week, because it shows what the system can achieve."},{"l":"B","t":"Whichever figure a competitor advertises for its own assistant in its public marketing material."},{"l":"C","t":"A task-success threshold derived from eval results and acceptance criteria agreed before launch."},{"l":"D","t":"99.9%, because a number of that kind sounds professional to the sponsor and signals ambition."}],"a":["C"],"r":"An SLA names what is measured, what counts as a breach, and what happens next. Thresholds must trace to something tangible: latency to user expectations, availability to business criticality, quality to eval results.","x":["Incorrect. The best week observed in a pilot overstates what the system can sustain, so the SLA would be breached routinely.","Incorrect. A competitor's advertised figure has no link to your system's evals or your users' needs.","Correct. A threshold tied to eval results and acceptance criteria agreed before launch is something you can measure and defend.","Incorrect. A round number chosen to sound ambitious is not traceable to eval evidence, and may be unattainable."],"df":"medium","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P94","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A deployment's eval score drifted downward for four weeks. Error rate stayed flat so no alert fired, and the problem was found only at the quarterly review. What was missing?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A decision layer mapping each signal, including slow drifts, to a trigger, an owner, and an action."},{"l":"B","t":"A larger model tier, so that the quality of the answers stops drifting downward over time."},{"l":"C","t":"A rule that every request is reviewed manually by an engineer before it is delivered to users."},{"l":"D","t":"More dashboards that display the same metrics in additional views for the stakeholders to inspect."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Monitoring is not a feedback loop. Observability supplies signals; the loop decides which matter via signals → triage → decide → act → review, with triggers and owners defined before launch.","x":["Correct. Monitoring supplies signals, but a decision layer maps each signal, including slow drifts, to a trigger, an owner and an action. Without it a four-week drift goes unnoticed.","Incorrect. A larger model does not stop drift, and the missing piece was a process for acting on signals.","Incorrect. Reviewing every request manually does not scale and does not provide a trigger for slow quality drift.","Incorrect. More dashboards of the same metrics add views but not triggers, owners or actions."],"df":"medium","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P95","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"Which production signal should be escalated to a stakeholder review?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Daily token consumption that stays inside the band forecast during the planning phase."},{"l":"B","t":"A single user's typo in a prompt that the model handled without any difficulty."},{"l":"C","t":"A diagnosed, sustained quality decline that threatens the agreed quality SLA."},{"l":"D","t":"A one-minute spike of 429 errors that was fully absorbed by retries with backoff."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Triage sorts signals into internal monitoring, architect review, stakeholder review, and noise. Transient, absorbed errors and in-forecast consumption are noise or internal monitoring; a confirmed, sustained SLA threat belongs with the stakeholder.","x":["Incorrect. Consumption within the forecast band is normal and belongs in internal monitoring, not a stakeholder review.","Incorrect. One user's typo that the model handled is noise and needs no escalation.","Correct. A diagnosed, sustained quality decline that threatens the agreed quality SLA is what a stakeholder needs to see.","Incorrect. A brief 429 spike absorbed by retries with backoff is a transient error that the system handled, so it is noise."],"df":"medium","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P96","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A residency-regulated deployment relied only on alerts. Months later an auditor asked for proof that residency had been confirmed regularly. What was missing?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Scheduled review checkpoints wired to triggers and owners at design time, with evidence retained."},{"l":"B","t":"A marketing statement from the platform vendor saying that the service is compliant with residency rules."},{"l":"C","t":"A larger model for the compliance checks, so that residency problems are noticed more reliably."},{"l":"D","t":"A higher alert threshold, so that fewer alerts are raised and each one carries more weight."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Some regulated reviews must run on a schedule even when nothing has gone wrong. If a checkpoint is never wired to a trigger, a violation can run for weeks before routine review catches it.","x":["Correct. Regulated reviews must run on a schedule even when nothing seems wrong. Checkpoints wired to triggers and owners at design time, with evidence retained, give the auditor proof.","Incorrect. A vendor's marketing statement is not evidence that your deployment was checked regularly.","Incorrect. A larger model does not create the scheduled checkpoints or the records an auditor asks for.","Incorrect. Raising the alert threshold produces fewer alerts and gives no scheduled evidence of review."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P102","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"Six months after launch an assistant's quality is acceptable, but cost per request has tripled as conversations grew longer, and the monolithic context strategy is the cause. Which judgement fits the 'decide' step of the feedback loop?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"**Re-architect rather than iterate:** the context strategy is the cause, so prompt or threshold changes cannot reach it."},{"l":"B","t":"**Iterate:** tune the prompt wording and the `max_tokens` cap, then re-measure the cost at the end of next quarter."},{"l":"C","t":"**Iterate:** move to a cheaper model tier, which lowers cost without changing anything else about the design."},{"l":"D","t":"**Neither:** rising cost at higher volume is expected, so it should be reported to the sponsor and accepted."}],"a":["A"],"r":"The loop runs signals, triage, decide, act, review, and the decide step separates what the team can iterate on from a cause that sits in the architecture. When the cost driver is the context strategy itself, tuning around it does not reach the cause and the design decision has to be reopened with the stakeholder.","x":["Correct. The context strategy is the cause, so changes to prompts or thresholds cannot reach it. The design decision needs to be reopened.","Incorrect. Tuning wording and a token cap does not change a monolithic context strategy, and waiting a quarter to re-measure delays the real decision.","Incorrect. A cheaper tier lowers the unit price but leaves the growing context unchanged, and it may reduce quality that is currently acceptable.","Incorrect. A tripled cost per request caused by the architecture is not simply expected. It needs a decision rather than acceptance."],"df":"hard","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P273","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A discovery workshop for a claims assistant includes only the business sponsor and the development team. Which gap is most likely to cause problems later?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Too few developers, which will slow the build."},{"l":"B","t":"Missing compliance, operations, and end-user voices, whose requirements will surface late."},{"l":"C","t":"Too many questions about current processes, which wastes the sponsor's time."},{"l":"D","t":"No vendor representative in the room to confirm what the model can and cannot do for this particular use case."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Compliance, operations, and end users each hold requirements the sponsor may not mention. Missing them in discovery means late surprises.","x":["Incorrect. The number of developers is not the main risk in discovery.","Correct. Compliance, operations and end users each hold requirements the sponsor may not mention, and missing them means late surprises.","Incorrect. Asking about current processes is valuable discovery and not a waste of time.","Incorrect. A vendor representative is not what is missing. The missing perspectives are those of the people who use and govern the process."],"df":"easy","ob":"Conduct structured discovery and requirement gathering"},
{"id":"P275","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A sponsor says, 'The assistant must be 100% accurate.' How should the architect respond?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Translate it into acceptable error rates per error type, and the controls, such as review, that catch errors."},{"l":"B","t":"Accept it as written and include it in the statement of work."},{"l":"C","t":"Refuse the project, since no AI system can be 100% accurate."},{"l":"D","t":"Agree to the requirement, and plan to use the largest model tier available so that the accuracy target is met in production."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Translate absolute language into measurable requirements: tolerable error rates by type, and the controls that catch errors. Refusing or accepting it as written both fail the sponsor.","x":["Correct. Translate the absolute into acceptable error rates per error type, together with controls such as review that catch errors.","Incorrect. Accepting 100% accuracy in a statement of work commits the team to something that cannot be guaranteed.","Incorrect. Refusing the project fails the sponsor, when the requirement can be translated into measurable terms.","Incorrect. A largest tier does not deliver 100% accuracy, so agreeing to the requirement is still wrong."],"df":"medium","ob":"Conduct structured discovery and requirement gathering"},
{"id":"P278","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"Legal wants every AI reply reviewed by a person; operations wants full automation. Both report to the same sponsor. What should the architect do?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Side with operations, since automation is the purpose of the project."},{"l":"B","t":"Surface the trade-off, propose risk-tiered review with costs for each option, and get the decision owner to decide."},{"l":"C","t":"Side with legal, since compliance requirements always outrank efficiency goals and the sponsor will expect that ordering."},{"l":"D","t":"Build both versions and let the teams use whichever they prefer."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Conflicting requirements are decisions for the accountable owner. Surface the trade-off, offer options with costs, and get a decision recorded.","x":["Incorrect. Siding with operations ignores a legal requirement without an informed decision from the owner.","Correct. Surface the trade-off, propose risk-tiered review with the costs of each option, and have the decision owner decide.","Incorrect. Compliance is important, but an architect should not decide a business trade-off alone, and full review may not be needed for every reply.","Incorrect. Building both versions wastes effort and leaves the underlying decision unresolved."],"df":"medium","ob":"Communicate architectural decisions and trade-offs"},
{"id":"P280","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"An executive asks, 'Will the AI ever get it wrong?' Which answer best supports a sound decision?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"'No, the model is very accurate and has been tested.'"},{"l":"B","t":"'Yes, AI systems sometimes hallucinate, so there will always be some risk of a wrong answer in production whatever we do about it.'"},{"l":"C","t":"'In testing it is wrong on about 3 in 100 requests; this control catches most of those, and here is the cost of the rest.'"},{"l":"D","t":"'It depends on many technical factors that are hard to explain.'"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Executives need probabilistic behaviour expressed in business terms: rates, controls, and residual cost. Vague or absolute answers do not support a decision.","x":["Incorrect. 'No' is an absolute that is not true and sets up a false expectation.","Incorrect. This is vague and gives the executive no figures or controls to decide on.","Correct. It states the error rate in business terms, what catches most errors, and the cost of the remainder, which supports a decision.","Incorrect. An answer that avoids the question leaves the executive unable to decide."],"df":"medium","ob":"Communicate architectural decisions and trade-offs"},
{"id":"P282","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A sponsor wants a full multi-agent assistant live in four weeks. Your estimate is twelve weeks for that scope. What is the best response?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree to four weeks and try to work faster."},{"l":"B","t":"Offer a phased plan: what can safely ship in four weeks, and what follows, with the risks of compressing it."},{"l":"C","t":"Refuse to start until the sponsor accepts twelve weeks."},{"l":"D","t":"Agree to four weeks, but skip evaluation and testing so that the full multi-agent scope fits within the sponsor's deadline."}],"a":["B"],"r":"Offer a phased path that fits the deadline safely, and state the risks of compressing it. Cutting evaluation hides risk rather than removing it.","x":["Incorrect. Agreeing and hoping to work faster hides a real risk and probably leads to a missed date or poor quality.","Correct. A phased plan shows what can safely ship in four weeks and what follows, and states the risks of compressing the scope.","Incorrect. Refusing to start is unhelpful and ignores that part of the scope can be delivered earlier.","Incorrect. Skipping evaluation and testing hides risk rather than removing it, and it leads to failures after launch."],"df":"medium","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P284","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A sponsor wants a quality SLA for an assistant. Which TWO elements make it enforceable? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A statement that quality will be 'excellent'."},{"l":"B","t":"A target chosen to impress the sponsor's leadership."},{"l":"C","t":"A defined measurement method, sample, and window."},{"l":"D","t":"An exclusion of every case where the model is wrong."},{"l":"E","t":"An agreed response when the threshold is breached."}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"An SLA names what is measured, how, and what happens on breach. Adjectives and exclusions of every failure make it unenforceable.","x":["Incorrect. A statement that quality will be 'excellent' cannot be measured or enforced.","Incorrect. A target chosen to impress rather than from evidence is not defensible or enforceable.","Correct. A defined measurement method, sample and window make the SLA measurable.","Incorrect. Excluding every case where the model is wrong makes the SLA meaningless.","Correct. An agreed response when the threshold is breached makes the SLA enforceable."],"df":"hard","ob":"Manage stakeholder feedback loops and expectation alignment (including SLAs)"},
{"id":"P288","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"The model version a production system uses is scheduled for retirement in four months. What is the best lifecycle response?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Wait until the retirement date, then switch to whichever model the provider recommends as the replacement at that time."},{"l":"B","t":"Switch to the newest model today without testing, to finish early."},{"l":"C","t":"Ask the provider to keep the old model running for this customer."},{"l":"D","t":"Plan the migration: evaluate candidate models on the golden dataset, schedule the change, and tell stakeholders."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Model retirement is a planned lifecycle event. Evaluate candidates, schedule the change, and communicate, rather than switching blind.","x":["Incorrect. Waiting until retirement and switching to a recommended model leaves no time to evaluate it or tell stakeholders.","Incorrect. Switching to the newest model untested risks quality regressions with no evidence.","Incorrect. Retirement dates are set by the provider and a model cannot be kept running for one customer, so this is not a plan.","Correct. Treat retirement as a planned lifecycle event. Evaluate candidates on the golden dataset, schedule the change and tell stakeholders."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P292","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"Which THREE items belong in a production-readiness checklist for a Claude application? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Evals pass the agreed thresholds on the current golden dataset."},{"l":"B","t":"Monitoring and alerts are in place, each with a named owner."},{"l":"C","t":"The sponsor has approved a demo."},{"l":"D","t":"A tested rollback plan exists."},{"l":"E","t":"The largest model tier is selected."}],"a":["A","B","D"],"r":"Readiness means evidence of quality, owned monitoring, and a tested way back. A demo and a large model are not readiness.","x":["Correct. Evals passing the agreed thresholds on the current golden dataset is evidence of quality.","Correct. Monitoring and alerts with named owners mean problems are noticed and someone is responsible.","Incorrect. A sponsor's approval of a demo is not evidence that the application is ready for production.","Correct. A tested rollback plan gives a way back if the release causes problems.","Incorrect. The largest tier is not a readiness criterion. The model should be chosen by evals, cost and latency."],"df":"hard","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P293","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A pilot assistant is being retired. What must the retirement plan include?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Turning off the user interface; nothing else is needed."},{"l":"B","t":"Keep all of the pilot's data indefinitely, in case it is needed later for a future project or an audit."},{"l":"C","t":"Asking the model to stop responding."},{"l":"D","t":"Handling stored data under retention rules, notifying users, and revoking keys and integrations."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Retirement covers data under retention rules, user communication, and revoking credentials and integrations, so that nothing lingers.","x":["Incorrect. Turning off the interface leaves data, keys and integrations in place.","Incorrect. Keeping all pilot data indefinitely conflicts with retention rules and increases risk.","Incorrect. Asking the model to stop responding does not retire the system or its data and credentials.","Correct. Retirement covers stored data under retention rules, notifying users, and revoking keys and integrations."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P298","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"Which risk-register entry is most useful?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"'Retrieval quality may drop as the corpus grows; likelihood medium; impact high; mitigation: weekly recall@k check; owner named; trigger: recall below 0.85.'"},{"l":"B","t":"'AI risk: high. The team will monitor the model closely and act quickly if anything goes wrong after launch.'"},{"l":"C","t":"'The model might hallucinate sometimes, which could affect customers; the team should keep this in mind during the project.'"},{"l":"D","t":"'Risks to be discussed at the next steering meeting, once the system is in production and real data is available.'"}],"a":["A"],"r":"A useful risk entry names likelihood, impact, mitigation, owner, and a measurable trigger. Vague entries cannot be acted on.","x":["Correct. It names the risk, likelihood, impact, mitigation, an owner and a measurable trigger, so someone can act on it.","Incorrect. 'AI risk: high' with a promise to monitor is vague and has no owner, measure or trigger.","Incorrect. 'Keep this in mind' gives no mitigation, owner or trigger.","Incorrect. Deferring the discussion until production means the risk is not managed during the build."],"df":"hard","ob":"Document architectures and provide implementation guidance"},
{"id":"P299","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"Mid-project, the sponsor asks to add Hindi and Tamil support. What is the best way to handle the request?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agree immediately and add it to the current sprint."},{"l":"B","t":"Refuse the request, since the scope was agreed and fixed at the start of the project and cannot change now."},{"l":"C","t":"Assess the impact on scope, evals, cost, and timeline, and agree the change through change control."},{"l":"D","t":"Add the languages quietly without updating the plan."}],"a":["C"],"r":"Change control assesses impact on scope, evals, cost, and timeline before agreeing. Language support needs new eval slices, not just a configuration change.","x":["Incorrect. Agreeing at once and adding it to the sprint ignores the cost, the timeline and the evals needed.","Incorrect. Refusing outright is inflexible. A changed requirement should be assessed and not simply rejected.","Correct. Assess the impact on scope, evals, cost and timeline and agree the change through change control.","Incorrect. Adding languages quietly without updating the plan leaves them untested and untracked."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support lifecycle phases (discovery, design, handoff, monitoring, iteration)"},
{"id":"P103","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"An organisation wants a code-review procedure used identically by all departments, centrally revocable, with version-controlled updates and rollback. Which distribution mechanism is the strongest fit?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A Claude Code project Skill kept in the repository of a single team and shared by copy."},{"l":"B","t":"Each developer installs the Skill manually from a shared link and updates it when they remember."},{"l":"C","t":"An org-provisioned Skill, because it offers native version pinning and an automatic rollback path."},{"l":"D","t":"A plugin assigned to a group or org, with version-controlled updates from a connected repository."}],"a":["D"],"r":"Plugins give group and org targeting, install preferences, and version-controlled updates with a path back. Org-provisioned Skills reach everyone but have no native version pinning or rollback, and project Skills are scoped to the repositories that carry them.","x":["Incorrect. A Skill in one team's repository shared by copy is scoped to that repository and spreads as unmanaged copies.","Incorrect. Manual installs from a shared link leave every developer on a different version, with no central control.","Incorrect. Org-provisioned Skills reach everyone but have no native version pinning or rollback path.","Correct. A plugin assigned to a group or org gives central targeting and revocation, version-controlled updates from a connected repository, and a path back."],"df":"medium","ob":"Configure Claude tools and environments for teams (e.g., Claude Code)"},
{"id":"P107","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Which TWO failure modes should a team rollout of AI tooling be designed to prevent? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Appointing one champion in each department to prove the workflow before wider rollout."},{"l":"B","t":"Stalling at basic chat, where the team never advances to repository-aware assistance, tool use, or packaged Skills."},{"l":"C","t":"Lumpy adoption, where a few developers use the tools heavily and the rest barely touch them."},{"l":"D","t":"Starting every team member from the same shared baseline configuration and permission posture."},{"l":"E","t":"Setting model allowlists, rate limits, and per-user spend caps as part of the team setup."}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Champion-and-batch rollout spreads usage deliberately, and enablement beyond first-step access prevents stalling. A shared configuration and spend posture are part of good setup, not failure modes.","x":["Incorrect. One champion per department proving the workflow first is a good rollout practice, not a failure mode.","Correct. Stalling at basic chat means the team never reaches repository-aware assistance, tool use or Skills, so enablement must go beyond first-step access.","Correct. Lumpy adoption, with a few heavy users and many who barely use the tools, is a failure mode that a deliberate rollout should prevent.","Incorrect. A shared baseline configuration and permission posture is part of good setup, not a failure mode.","Incorrect. Allowlists, rate limits and per-user spend caps are sound controls, not something to prevent."],"df":"medium","ob":"Improve developer workflows using AI-assisted tooling"},
{"id":"P108","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"AI-generated code passes all tests, but the submitting developer cannot explain how it handles inputs outside the test set. Which verification-checklist dimension has failed?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Security only, because a developer who cannot explain the code has probably left a vulnerability in it."},{"l":"B","t":"Maintainability only, because the formatting differs slightly from the team's usual style."},{"l":"C","t":"Human understanding: the author must be able to explain the code, including untested inputs."},{"l":"D","t":"Correctness, because the tests failed and the change should therefore never have reached review."}],"a":["C"],"r":"The four dimensions are correctness, security, maintainability, and human understanding. Green tests are not understanding; this gate stops judgment erosion from reaching production.","x":["Incorrect. Nothing in the scenario shows a vulnerability. The failed dimension is that the author does not understand the code, which is not only a security matter.","Incorrect. Style differences are not the issue here. The problem is the author's inability to explain behaviour on untested inputs.","Correct. Human understanding has failed. Passing tests does not mean the author can explain how the code handles inputs outside the test set.","Incorrect. The tests passed, so correctness on the tested cases is not what failed. The gap is understanding."],"df":"medium","ob":"Improve developer workflows using AI-assisted tooling"},
{"id":"P109","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"Latency has spiked in a production Claude system with no code change. Which TWO first actions are most appropriate? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Rotate the API keys, because stale credentials can slow down every request."},{"l":"B","t":"Confirm prompt-cache behaviour, since a cache that stopped hitting raises latency and cost."},{"l":"C","t":"Move all traffic to a larger model tier, which usually speeds responses up on its own."},{"l":"D","t":"Rewrite the system prompt's tone and phrasing, since wording changes drive latency."},{"l":"E","t":"Use traces to find the slowest span, checking tokens per request and the slowest tool call."}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Latency spikes usually trace to context growth, a slow tool, or a cache that stopped hitting. The first action is to find the slowest span with telemetry and confirm cache behaviour before changing anything.","x":["Incorrect. Stale credentials cause authentication errors, not slow responses, so rotating keys is not a first step for latency.","Correct. A prompt cache that stopped hitting raises both latency and cost, so confirm cache behaviour early.","Incorrect. Larger tiers are usually slower, and moving all traffic is a big change before finding the cause.","Incorrect. Tone and phrasing changes do not drive latency, and changing the prompt before diagnosing is guesswork.","Correct. Traces show the slowest span, with tokens per request and the slowest tool call, so you fix the actual cause."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support debugging and operational issue resolution"},
{"id":"P110","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants three things from Claude Code: standing repository conventions every session should know, a release-notes procedure run the same way each time, and codebase exploration that runs in its own context without cluttering the main thread. Which mapping is correct?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"All three as MCP servers, so that each one can be reused from other Claude clients at a later date."},{"l":"B","t":"Conventions in `CLAUDE.md`, release notes as a Skill, and exploration as a subagent with its own context."},{"l":"C","t":"All three in `CLAUDE.md`, so that everything the agent might need is loaded at the start of every session."},{"l":"D","t":"Conventions as a Skill, the release-notes procedure in `CLAUDE.md`, and exploration handled by an MCP server."}],"a":["B"],"r":"`CLAUDE.md` carries persistent context that applies to every task; Skills package repeatable procedures loaded on demand so the main context stays lean; subagents give bounded work an isolated context window and, where needed, a restricted tool entry point. MCP is for a tool entry point that must be reachable from more than one client.","x":["Incorrect. MCP is for a tool entry point reachable from more than one client. Conventions, procedures and exploration are not what MCP is for.","Correct. CLAUDE.md carries persistent conventions, a Skill packages a repeatable procedure loaded on demand, and a subagent gives exploration its own context.","Incorrect. Putting everything in CLAUDE.md loads it into every session and clutters the main context, and it cannot isolate exploration.","Incorrect. This swaps the roles. Conventions belong in CLAUDE.md, procedures in a Skill, and exploration in a subagent rather than an MCP server."],"df":"medium","ob":"Configure Claude tools and environments for teams (e.g., Claude Code)"},
{"id":"P304","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A repository contains a `.env` file with credentials. The team wants Claude Code never to read it. Which configuration is most direct?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A deny rule for reading that file in the permission settings."},{"l":"B","t":"A line in `CLAUDE.md` asking Claude not to open the file."},{"l":"C","t":"Renaming the file so that its name does not suggest secrets."},{"l":"D","t":"Moving the file to the repository root so that it is more visible."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Permission deny rules are enforced. `CLAUDE.md` is context, not enforcement, and renaming or moving the file changes nothing.","x":["Correct. A permission deny rule for reading that file is enforced by Claude Code.","Incorrect. A line in CLAUDE.md is context, not enforcement, so Claude could still open the file.","Incorrect. Renaming the file does not stop Claude reading it.","Incorrect. Moving the file to the repository root makes it more visible and does not restrict access."],"df":"medium","ob":"Configure Claude tools and environments for teams (e.g., Claude Code)"},
{"id":"P305","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team wants every developer to have the same MCP servers when they work in a repository with Claude Code. Some servers need API tokens. Which TWO practices fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask each developer to add the servers by hand following a wiki page."},{"l":"B","t":"Commit a project-scoped MCP configuration file to the repository."},{"l":"C","t":"Reference tokens through environment variables rather than committing them."},{"l":"D","t":"Commit the API tokens in the configuration file so that setup is automatic."},{"l":"E","t":"Share one developer's user-level configuration by email."}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"A project-scoped MCP configuration committed to the repository gives everyone the same servers, and environment variables keep secrets out of version control.","x":["Incorrect. Manual setup from a wiki page leads to inconsistent configurations between developers.","Correct. A project-scoped MCP configuration file committed to the repository gives every developer the same servers.","Correct. Referencing tokens through environment variables keeps secrets out of version control while the configuration stays shared.","Incorrect. Committing tokens exposes secrets to everyone with repository access and in its history.","Incorrect. Emailing one developer's user-level configuration spreads personal settings and possibly secrets, and is not a shared repository setup."],"df":"hard","ob":"Configure Claude tools and environments for teams (e.g., Claude Code)"},
{"id":"P312","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A production agent intermittently reports 'tool call failed' on a supplier lookup. What is the best first step?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use traces to find failing calls, inspect their inputs and the tool's logs, and reproduce with the same inputs."},{"l":"B","t":"Switch to a larger model, which will call the tool correctly."},{"l":"C","t":"Remove the supplier tool until the problem goes away."},{"l":"D","t":"Add a line to the system prompt asking the agent to retry the supplier lookup until it works, however many attempts that takes."}],"a":["A"],"r":"Debug from evidence: find the failing spans, inspect inputs and tool logs, and reproduce. Model changes and prompt nudges guess at the cause.","x":["Correct. Use traces to find the failing calls, inspect their inputs and the tool's logs, and reproduce with the same inputs.","Incorrect. Switching to a larger model guesses at the cause and does not fix a tool failure.","Incorrect. Removing the tool takes away the feature instead of finding why the call fails.","Incorrect. Asking the agent to retry endlessly can hide the failure and waste calls, without finding the cause."],"df":"medium","ob":"Support debugging and operational issue resolution"}
];
